window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2024-06-1", "source": "2024年06月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence “Nowadays, cultivating independent learning ability is becoming increasingly crucial for personal development.” You can make comments, cite examples or use your personal experiences to develop your essay. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words. (You should copy the sentence given in quotes at the beginning of your essay.)", "sample": "Nowadays, cultivating independent learning ability is becoming increasingly crucial for personal development.\nAs knowledge updates at an unprecedented pace, the skills we master at school can no longer carry us through\nan entire career, which makes the capacity to learn on our own a decisive advantage.\nTo begin with, self-directed learning keeps us competitive. Those who can teach themselves new tools quickly\nadapt to changing workplaces, while those who wait to be taught inevitably fall behind. Take my own\nexperience: when our university suddenly shifted to online courses, I taught myself data analysis through free\ntutorials, and that skill later helped me stand out in an internship. Moreover, lifelong learning nurtures\nindependent thinking, enabling us to judge information critically rather than swallow it blindly.\nIn conclusion, independent learning is far more than an academic skill; it is a lifelong engine that drives\npersonal growth. Therefore, we should consciously develop this ability and treat every day as a chance to learn."}, "translation": {"prompt": "中国的传统婚礼习俗历史悠久，从周朝开始就逐渐形成了一套完整的婚礼仪式，有些一直沿用至今。如今的中式婚礼习俗已有很大变化，但婚礼庆典仍然十分隆重。婚礼场地经过精心装饰，以象征喜庆 (jubilance) 的红色为主色调，摆放着许多祝愿新人幸福的物件。在婚礼上，新人要拜天地 (bow to Heaven and Earth)、拜父母和相互对拜，然后设宴招待宾客，并向宾客敬酒致谢。今天，许多年轻人依然钟情于传统的中式婚礼，体验独特而美好的中国式浪漫。", "sample": "China's traditional wedding customs have a long history. A complete set of wedding ceremonies gradually took shape as early as the Zhou Dynasty, some of which have been followed to this day. Today's Chinese wedding customs have changed greatly, but the wedding celebration remains very grand. The wedding venue is elaborately decorated, with red—the color symbolizing jubilance—as the main tone, and many objects wishing the couple happiness are placed around it. At the wedding, the couple bow to Heaven and Earth, to their parents and to each other, then hold a banquet to entertain the guests and propose a toast to thank them. Today, many young people are still fond of traditional Chinese weddings, experiencing the unique and wonderful Chinese-style romance."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2024-06-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "It's quite remarkable how different genres of music can spark unique feelings, emotions, and memories. Studies have shown that music can reduce stress and anxiety before surgeries and we are all attracted toward our own unique life soundtrack.\nIf you're looking to  __26__  stress, you might want to give classical music a try.\nThe sounds of classical music produce a calming effect letting  __27__  pleasure-inducing dopamine(多巴胺) in the brain that helps control attention, learning and emotional responses. It can also turn down the body's stress response, resulting in an overall happier mood. It turns out a pleasant mood can lead to  __28__  in a person's thinking.\nAlthough there are many great  __29__  of classical music like Bach, Beethoven and Handel, none of these artists' music seems to have the same health effects as Mozart's does. According to researchers, listening to Mozart can increase brain wave activity and improve  __30__  function. Another study found that the distinctive features of Mozart's music trigger parts of the brain that are responsible for high-level mental functions. Even maternity  __31__  use Mozart to help newborn babies adapt to life outside of the mother's belly.\nIt has been found that listening to classical music  __32__  reduces a person's blood pressure. Researchers believe that the calming sounds of classical music may help your heart  __33__  from stress. Classical music can also be a great tool to help people who have trouble sleeping. One study found that students who had trouble sleeping slept better while they were listening to classical music.\nWhether classical music is something that you listen to on a regular basis or not, it wouldn't  __34__  to take time out of your day to listen to music that you find  __35__  . You will be surprised at how good it makes you feel and the potentially positive change in your health.", "matchIntro": "The Curious Case of the Tree That Owns Itself", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "In the city of Athens, Georgia, there exists a rather curious local landmark—a large white oak that is almost universally stated to own itself. Because of this, it is considered one of the most famous trees in the world. So how did this tree come to own itself and the land around it?"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Sometime in the 19th century a Georgian called Colonel William Jackson reportedly took a liking to the said tree and endeavored to protect it from any danger. As to why he loved it so, the earliest documented account of this story is an anonymously written front page article in the Athens Weekly Banner published on August 12, 1890. It states, \"Col. Jackson had watched the tree grow from his childhood, and grew to love it almost as he would a human. Its luxuriant leaves and sturdy limbs had often protected him from the heavy rains, and out of its highest branches he had many a time gotten the eggs of the feathered singers. He watched its growth, and when reaching a ripe old age he saw the tree standing in its magnificent proportions, he was pained to think that after his death it would fall into the hands of those who might destroy it.\""}, {"label": "C", "text": "Towards this end, Jackson transferred by means of a deed ownership of the tree and a little land around it to the tree itself. The deed read, \"W. H. Jackson for and in consideration of the great affection which he bears the said tree, and his great desire to see it protected has conveyed unto the said oak tree entire possession of itself and of all land within eight feet of it on all sides.\""}, {"label": "D", "text": "In time, the tree came to be something of a tourist attraction, known as The Tree That Owns Itself. However, in the early 20th century, the tree started showing signs of its slow death, with little that could be done about it. Father time comes for us all eventually, even our often long lived, tall and leafy fellow custodians(看管者) of Earth. Finally, on October 9, 1942, the over 30 meter tall and 200-400 year old tree fell, rumor has it, as a result of a severe windstorm and/or via having previously died and its roots rotted."}, {"label": "E", "text": "About four years later, members of the Junior Ladies Garden Club (who'd tended to the tree before its unfortunate death) tracked down a small tree grown from a nut taken from the original tree. And so it was that on October 9, 1946, under the direction of Professor Roy Bowden of the College of Agriculture at the University of Georgia, this little tree was transplanted to the location of its ancestor. A couple of months later, an official ceremony was held featuring none other than the Mayor of Athens, Robert L McWhorter, to commemorate the occasion."}, {"label": "F", "text": "This new tree became known as The Son of the Tree That Owns Itself and it was assumed that, as the original tree's heir, it naturally inherited the land it stood on. Of course, there are many dozens of other trees known to exist descending from the original, as people taking a nut from it to grow elsewhere was a certainty. That said, to date, none of the original tree's other children have petitioned the courts for their share of the land, so it seems all good. In any event, The Son of the Tree That Owns Itself still stands today, though often referred to simply as The Tree That Owns Itself."}, {"label": "G", "text": "This all brings us around to whether Jackson ever actually gave legal ownership of the tree to itself in the first place and whether such a deed is legally binding."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Well, to begin with, it turns out Jackson only spent about three years of his life in Athens, starting at the age of 43 from 1829 to 1832, sort of dismissing the idea that he loved the tree from spending time under it as a child and watching it grow, and then worrying about what would happen to it after he died. Further, an extensive search of land ownership records in Athens does not seem to indicate Jackson ever owned the land the tree sits on."}, {"label": "I", "text": "He did live on a lot of land directly next to it for those three years, but whether he owned that land or not isn't clear. Whatever the case, in 1832 a four acre parcel, which included the land the tree was on and the neighboring land Jackson lived on, among others, was sold to University professor Malthus A Ward. In the transaction, Ward was required to pay Jackson a sum of $1,200 (about $31, 000 today), either for the property itself or simply in compensation for improvements Jackson had made on the lot. In the end, whether he ever owned the neighboring lot or was simply allowed to use it while he allegedly worked at the University, he definitely never owned the lot the tree grew on, which is the most important bit for the topic at hand."}, {"label": "J", "text": "After Professor Ward purchased the land, Jackson and his family purchased a 655 acre parcel a few miles away and moved there. Ten years later, in 1844, Jackson seemed to have come into financial difficulties and had his little plantation seized by the Clarke County Sheriff's office and auctioned off to settle the mortgage. Thus, had he owned some land in Athens itself, including the land the tree sat on, presumably he would have sold it to raise funds or otherwise had it taken as well."}, {"label": "K", "text": "And whatever the case there, Jackson would have known property taxes needed to be paid on the deeded land for the tree to be truly secure in its future. Yet no account or record indicates any trust or the like was set up to facilitate this."}, {"label": "L", "text": "On top of all this, there is no hard evidence such a deed ever existed, despite the fact that deed records in Athens go back many decades before Jackson's death in 1876 and that it was supposed to have existed in 1890 in the archives according to the original anonymous news reporter who claims to have seen it."}, {"label": "M", "text": "As you might imagine from all of this, few give credit to this side of the story. So how did all of this come about then?"}, {"label": "N", "text": "It is speculated to have been invented by the imagination of the said anonymous author at the Athens Weekly Banner in the aforementioned 1890 front page article titled \"Deeded to Itself\", which by the way contained several elements that are much more easily proved to be false. As to why the author would do this, it's speculated perhaps it was a 19th century version of a click-bait thought exercise on whether it would be legal for someone to deed such a non-conscious living thing to itself or not."}, {"label": "O", "text": "Whatever the case, the next known instance of the Tree That Owns Itself being mentioned wasn't until 1901 in the Centennial Edition of that same paper, the Athens Weekly Banner. This featured another account very clearly just copying the original article published about a decade before, only slightly reworded. The next account was in 1906, again in the Athens Weekly Banner, again very clearly copying the original account, only slightly reworded, the 19th century equivalent of re-posts when the audience has forgotten about the original."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "It is irrefutable that employees know the difference between right and wrong. So why don't more employees intervene when they see someone exhibiting at-risk behavior in the workplace?\nThere are a number of factors that influence whether people intervene. First, they need to be able to see a risky situation beginning to unfold. Second, the company's culture needs to make them feel safe to speak up. And third, they need to have the communication skills to say something effectively.\nThis is not strictly a workplace problem; it's a growing problem off the job too. Every day people witness things on the street and choose to stand idly by. This is known as the bystander effect—the more people who witness an event, the less likely anyone in that group is to help the victim. The psychology behind this is called diffusion of responsibility. Basically, the larger the crowd, the more people assume that someone else will take care of it—meaning no one effectively intervenes or acts in a moment of need.\nThis crowd mentality is strong enough for people to evade their known responsibilities. But it's not only frontline workers who don't make safety interventions in the workplace. There are also instances where supervisors do not intervene either.\nWhen a group of employees sees unsafe behavior not being addressed at a leadership level it creates the precedent that this is how these situations should be addressed, thus defining the safety culture for everyone.\nDespite the fact that workers are encouraged to intervene when they observe unsafe operations, this happens less than half of the time. Fear is the ultimate factor in not intervening. There is a fear of penalty, a fear that they'll have to do more work if they intervene. Unsuccessful attempts in the past are another strong contributing factor to why people don't intervene—they tend to prefer to defer that action to someone else for all future situations.\nOn many worksites, competent workers must be appointed. Part of their job is to intervene when workers perform a task without the proper equipment or if the conditions are unsafe. Competent workers are also required to stop work from continuing when there's a danger.\nSupervisors also play a critical role. Even if a competent person isn't required, supervisors need a broad set of skills to not only identify and alleviate workplace hazards but also build a safety climate within their team that supports intervening and open communication among them.\nBeyond competent workers and supervisors, it's important to educate everyone within the organization that they are obliged to intervene if they witness a possible unsafe act, whether you're a designated competent person, a supervisor or a frontline worker.", "47": "It is irrefutable that employees know the difference between right and wrong. So why don't more employees intervene when they see someone exhibiting at-risk behavior in the workplace?\nThere are a number of factors that influence whether people intervene. First, they need to be able to see a risky situation beginning to unfold. Second, the company's culture needs to make them feel safe to speak up. And third, they need to have the communication skills to say something effectively.\nThis is not strictly a workplace problem; it's a growing problem off the job too. Every day people witness things on the street and choose to stand idly by. This is known as the bystander effect—the more people who witness an event, the less likely anyone in that group is to help the victim. The psychology behind this is called diffusion of responsibility. Basically, the larger the crowd, the more people assume that someone else will take care of it—meaning no one effectively intervenes or acts in a moment of need.\nThis crowd mentality is strong enough for people to evade their known responsibilities. But it's not only frontline workers who don't make safety interventions in the workplace. There are also instances where supervisors do not intervene either.\nWhen a group of employees sees unsafe behavior not being addressed at a leadership level it creates the precedent that this is how these situations should be addressed, thus defining the safety culture for everyone.\nDespite the fact that workers are encouraged to intervene when they observe unsafe operations, this happens less than half of the time. Fear is the ultimate factor in not intervening. There is a fear of penalty, a fear that they'll have to do more work if they intervene. Unsuccessful attempts in the past are another strong contributing factor to why people don't intervene—they tend to prefer to defer that action to someone else for all future situations.\nOn many worksites, competent workers must be appointed. Part of their job is to intervene when workers perform a task without the proper equipment or if the conditions are unsafe. Competent workers are also required to stop work from continuing when there's a danger.\nSupervisors also play a critical role. Even if a competent person isn't required, supervisors need a broad set of skills to not only identify and alleviate workplace hazards but also build a safety climate within their team that supports intervening and open communication among them.\nBeyond competent workers and supervisors, it's important to educate everyone within the organization that they are obliged to intervene if they witness a possible unsafe act, whether you're a designated competent person, a supervisor or a frontline worker.", "48": "It is irrefutable that employees know the difference between right and wrong. So why don't more employees intervene when they see someone exhibiting at-risk behavior in the workplace?\nThere are a number of factors that influence whether people intervene. First, they need to be able to see a risky situation beginning to unfold. Second, the company's culture needs to make them feel safe to speak up. And third, they need to have the communication skills to say something effectively.\nThis is not strictly a workplace problem; it's a growing problem off the job too. Every day people witness things on the street and choose to stand idly by. This is known as the bystander effect—the more people who witness an event, the less likely anyone in that group is to help the victim. The psychology behind this is called diffusion of responsibility. Basically, the larger the crowd, the more people assume that someone else will take care of it—meaning no one effectively intervenes or acts in a moment of need.\nThis crowd mentality is strong enough for people to evade their known responsibilities. But it's not only frontline workers who don't make safety interventions in the workplace. There are also instances where supervisors do not intervene either.\nWhen a group of employees sees unsafe behavior not being addressed at a leadership level it creates the precedent that this is how these situations should be addressed, thus defining the safety culture for everyone.\nDespite the fact that workers are encouraged to intervene when they observe unsafe operations, this happens less than half of the time. Fear is the ultimate factor in not intervening. There is a fear of penalty, a fear that they'll have to do more work if they intervene. Unsuccessful attempts in the past are another strong contributing factor to why people don't intervene—they tend to prefer to defer that action to someone else for all future situations.\nOn many worksites, competent workers must be appointed. Part of their job is to intervene when workers perform a task without the proper equipment or if the conditions are unsafe. Competent workers are also required to stop work from continuing when there's a danger.\nSupervisors also play a critical role. Even if a competent person isn't required, supervisors need a broad set of skills to not only identify and alleviate workplace hazards but also build a safety climate within their team that supports intervening and open communication among them.\nBeyond competent workers and supervisors, it's important to educate everyone within the organization that they are obliged to intervene if they witness a possible unsafe act, whether you're a designated competent person, a supervisor or a frontline worker.", "49": "It is irrefutable that employees know the difference between right and wrong. So why don't more employees intervene when they see someone exhibiting at-risk behavior in the workplace?\nThere are a number of factors that influence whether people intervene. First, they need to be able to see a risky situation beginning to unfold. Second, the company's culture needs to make them feel safe to speak up. And third, they need to have the communication skills to say something effectively.\nThis is not strictly a workplace problem; it's a growing problem off the job too. Every day people witness things on the street and choose to stand idly by. This is known as the bystander effect—the more people who witness an event, the less likely anyone in that group is to help the victim. The psychology behind this is called diffusion of responsibility. Basically, the larger the crowd, the more people assume that someone else will take care of it—meaning no one effectively intervenes or acts in a moment of need.\nThis crowd mentality is strong enough for people to evade their known responsibilities. But it's not only frontline workers who don't make safety interventions in the workplace. There are also instances where supervisors do not intervene either.\nWhen a group of employees sees unsafe behavior not being addressed at a leadership level it creates the precedent that this is how these situations should be addressed, thus defining the safety culture for everyone.\nDespite the fact that workers are encouraged to intervene when they observe unsafe operations, this happens less than half of the time. Fear is the ultimate factor in not intervening. There is a fear of penalty, a fear that they'll have to do more work if they intervene. Unsuccessful attempts in the past are another strong contributing factor to why people don't intervene—they tend to prefer to defer that action to someone else for all future situations.\nOn many worksites, competent workers must be appointed. Part of their job is to intervene when workers perform a task without the proper equipment or if the conditions are unsafe. Competent workers are also required to stop work from continuing when there's a danger.\nSupervisors also play a critical role. Even if a competent person isn't required, supervisors need a broad set of skills to not only identify and alleviate workplace hazards but also build a safety climate within their team that supports intervening and open communication among them.\nBeyond competent workers and supervisors, it's important to educate everyone within the organization that they are obliged to intervene if they witness a possible unsafe act, whether you're a designated competent person, a supervisor or a frontline worker.", "50": "It is irrefutable that employees know the difference between right and wrong. So why don't more employees intervene when they see someone exhibiting at-risk behavior in the workplace?\nThere are a number of factors that influence whether people intervene. First, they need to be able to see a risky situation beginning to unfold. Second, the company's culture needs to make them feel safe to speak up. And third, they need to have the communication skills to say something effectively.\nThis is not strictly a workplace problem; it's a growing problem off the job too. Every day people witness things on the street and choose to stand idly by. This is known as the bystander effect—the more people who witness an event, the less likely anyone in that group is to help the victim. The psychology behind this is called diffusion of responsibility. Basically, the larger the crowd, the more people assume that someone else will take care of it—meaning no one effectively intervenes or acts in a moment of need.\nThis crowd mentality is strong enough for people to evade their known responsibilities. But it's not only frontline workers who don't make safety interventions in the workplace. There are also instances where supervisors do not intervene either.\nWhen a group of employees sees unsafe behavior not being addressed at a leadership level it creates the precedent that this is how these situations should be addressed, thus defining the safety culture for everyone.\nDespite the fact that workers are encouraged to intervene when they observe unsafe operations, this happens less than half of the time. Fear is the ultimate factor in not intervening. There is a fear of penalty, a fear that they'll have to do more work if they intervene. Unsuccessful attempts in the past are another strong contributing factor to why people don't intervene—they tend to prefer to defer that action to someone else for all future situations.\nOn many worksites, competent workers must be appointed. Part of their job is to intervene when workers perform a task without the proper equipment or if the conditions are unsafe. Competent workers are also required to stop work from continuing when there's a danger.\nSupervisors also play a critical role. Even if a competent person isn't required, supervisors need a broad set of skills to not only identify and alleviate workplace hazards but also build a safety climate within their team that supports intervening and open communication among them.\nBeyond competent workers and supervisors, it's important to educate everyone within the organization that they are obliged to intervene if they witness a possible unsafe act, whether you're a designated competent person, a supervisor or a frontline worker.", "51": "The term \"environmentalist\" can mean different things. It used to refer to people trying to protect wildlife and natural ecosystems. In the 21st century, the term has evolved to capture the need to combat human-made climate change.\nThe distinction between these two strands of environmentalism is the cause of a split within the scientific community about nuclear energy.\nOn one side are purists who believe nuclear power isn't worth the risk and the exclusive solution to the climate crisis is renewable energy. The opposing side agrees that renewables are crucial, but says society needs an amount of power available to meet consumers' basic demands when the sun isn't shining and the wind isn't blowing. Nuclear energy, being far cleaner than oil, gas and coal, is a natural option, especially where hydroelectric capacity is limited.\nLeon Clarke, who helped author reports for the UN's Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change, isn't an uncritical supporter of nuclear energy, but says it's a valuable option to have if we're serious about reaching carbon neutrality.\n\"Core to all of this is the degree to which you think we can actually meet climate goals with 100% renewables,\" he said. \"If you don't believe we can do it, and you care about the climate, you are forced to think about something like nuclear.\"\nThe achievability of universal 100% renewability is similarly contentious. Cities such as Burlington, Vermont, have been \"100% renewable\" for years. But these cities often have small populations, occasionally still rely on fossil fuel energy and have significant renewable resources at their immediate disposal. Meanwhile, countries that manage to run off renewables typically do so thanks to extraordinary hydroelectric capabilities.\nGermany stands as the best case study for a large, industrialized country pushing into green energy. Chancellor Angela Merkel in 2011 announced Energiewende, an energy transition that would phase out nuclear and coal while phasing in renewables. Wind and solar power generation has increased over 400% since 2010, and renewables provided 46% of the country's electricity in 2019.\nBut progress has halted in recent years. The instability of renewables doesn't just mean energy is often not produced at night, but also that solar and wind can overwhelm the grid during the day, forcing utilities to pay customers to use their electricity. Lagging grid infrastructure struggles to transport this overabundance of green energy from Germany's north to its industrial south, meaning many factories still run on coal and gas. The political limit has also been reached in some places, with citizens meeting the construction of new wind turbines with loud protests.\nThe result is that Germany's greenhouse gas emissions have fallen by around 11.5% since 2010—slower than the EU average of 13.5%.", "52": "The term \"environmentalist\" can mean different things. It used to refer to people trying to protect wildlife and natural ecosystems. In the 21st century, the term has evolved to capture the need to combat human-made climate change.\nThe distinction between these two strands of environmentalism is the cause of a split within the scientific community about nuclear energy.\nOn one side are purists who believe nuclear power isn't worth the risk and the exclusive solution to the climate crisis is renewable energy. The opposing side agrees that renewables are crucial, but says society needs an amount of power available to meet consumers' basic demands when the sun isn't shining and the wind isn't blowing. Nuclear energy, being far cleaner than oil, gas and coal, is a natural option, especially where hydroelectric capacity is limited.\nLeon Clarke, who helped author reports for the UN's Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change, isn't an uncritical supporter of nuclear energy, but says it's a valuable option to have if we're serious about reaching carbon neutrality.\n\"Core to all of this is the degree to which you think we can actually meet climate goals with 100% renewables,\" he said. \"If you don't believe we can do it, and you care about the climate, you are forced to think about something like nuclear.\"\nThe achievability of universal 100% renewability is similarly contentious. Cities such as Burlington, Vermont, have been \"100% renewable\" for years. But these cities often have small populations, occasionally still rely on fossil fuel energy and have significant renewable resources at their immediate disposal. Meanwhile, countries that manage to run off renewables typically do so thanks to extraordinary hydroelectric capabilities.\nGermany stands as the best case study for a large, industrialized country pushing into green energy. Chancellor Angela Merkel in 2011 announced Energiewende, an energy transition that would phase out nuclear and coal while phasing in renewables. Wind and solar power generation has increased over 400% since 2010, and renewables provided 46% of the country's electricity in 2019.\nBut progress has halted in recent years. The instability of renewables doesn't just mean energy is often not produced at night, but also that solar and wind can overwhelm the grid during the day, forcing utilities to pay customers to use their electricity. Lagging grid infrastructure struggles to transport this overabundance of green energy from Germany's north to its industrial south, meaning many factories still run on coal and gas. The political limit has also been reached in some places, with citizens meeting the construction of new wind turbines with loud protests.\nThe result is that Germany's greenhouse gas emissions have fallen by around 11.5% since 2010—slower than the EU average of 13.5%.", "53": "The term \"environmentalist\" can mean different things. It used to refer to people trying to protect wildlife and natural ecosystems. In the 21st century, the term has evolved to capture the need to combat human-made climate change.\nThe distinction between these two strands of environmentalism is the cause of a split within the scientific community about nuclear energy.\nOn one side are purists who believe nuclear power isn't worth the risk and the exclusive solution to the climate crisis is renewable energy. The opposing side agrees that renewables are crucial, but says society needs an amount of power available to meet consumers' basic demands when the sun isn't shining and the wind isn't blowing. Nuclear energy, being far cleaner than oil, gas and coal, is a natural option, especially where hydroelectric capacity is limited.\nLeon Clarke, who helped author reports for the UN's Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change, isn't an uncritical supporter of nuclear energy, but says it's a valuable option to have if we're serious about reaching carbon neutrality.\n\"Core to all of this is the degree to which you think we can actually meet climate goals with 100% renewables,\" he said. \"If you don't believe we can do it, and you care about the climate, you are forced to think about something like nuclear.\"\nThe achievability of universal 100% renewability is similarly contentious. Cities such as Burlington, Vermont, have been \"100% renewable\" for years. But these cities often have small populations, occasionally still rely on fossil fuel energy and have significant renewable resources at their immediate disposal. Meanwhile, countries that manage to run off renewables typically do so thanks to extraordinary hydroelectric capabilities.\nGermany stands as the best case study for a large, industrialized country pushing into green energy. Chancellor Angela Merkel in 2011 announced Energiewende, an energy transition that would phase out nuclear and coal while phasing in renewables. Wind and solar power generation has increased over 400% since 2010, and renewables provided 46% of the country's electricity in 2019.\nBut progress has halted in recent years. The instability of renewables doesn't just mean energy is often not produced at night, but also that solar and wind can overwhelm the grid during the day, forcing utilities to pay customers to use their electricity. Lagging grid infrastructure struggles to transport this overabundance of green energy from Germany's north to its industrial south, meaning many factories still run on coal and gas. The political limit has also been reached in some places, with citizens meeting the construction of new wind turbines with loud protests.\nThe result is that Germany's greenhouse gas emissions have fallen by around 11.5% since 2010—slower than the EU average of 13.5%.", "54": "The term \"environmentalist\" can mean different things. It used to refer to people trying to protect wildlife and natural ecosystems. In the 21st century, the term has evolved to capture the need to combat human-made climate change.\nThe distinction between these two strands of environmentalism is the cause of a split within the scientific community about nuclear energy.\nOn one side are purists who believe nuclear power isn't worth the risk and the exclusive solution to the climate crisis is renewable energy. The opposing side agrees that renewables are crucial, but says society needs an amount of power available to meet consumers' basic demands when the sun isn't shining and the wind isn't blowing. Nuclear energy, being far cleaner than oil, gas and coal, is a natural option, especially where hydroelectric capacity is limited.\nLeon Clarke, who helped author reports for the UN's Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change, isn't an uncritical supporter of nuclear energy, but says it's a valuable option to have if we're serious about reaching carbon neutrality.\n\"Core to all of this is the degree to which you think we can actually meet climate goals with 100% renewables,\" he said. \"If you don't believe we can do it, and you care about the climate, you are forced to think about something like nuclear.\"\nThe achievability of universal 100% renewability is similarly contentious. Cities such as Burlington, Vermont, have been \"100% renewable\" for years. But these cities often have small populations, occasionally still rely on fossil fuel energy and have significant renewable resources at their immediate disposal. Meanwhile, countries that manage to run off renewables typically do so thanks to extraordinary hydroelectric capabilities.\nGermany stands as the best case study for a large, industrialized country pushing into green energy. Chancellor Angela Merkel in 2011 announced Energiewende, an energy transition that would phase out nuclear and coal while phasing in renewables. Wind and solar power generation has increased over 400% since 2010, and renewables provided 46% of the country's electricity in 2019.\nBut progress has halted in recent years. The instability of renewables doesn't just mean energy is often not produced at night, but also that solar and wind can overwhelm the grid during the day, forcing utilities to pay customers to use their electricity. Lagging grid infrastructure struggles to transport this overabundance of green energy from Germany's north to its industrial south, meaning many factories still run on coal and gas. The political limit has also been reached in some places, with citizens meeting the construction of new wind turbines with loud protests.\nThe result is that Germany's greenhouse gas emissions have fallen by around 11.5% since 2010—slower than the EU average of 13.5%.", "55": "The term \"environmentalist\" can mean different things. It used to refer to people trying to protect wildlife and natural ecosystems. In the 21st century, the term has evolved to capture the need to combat human-made climate change.\nThe distinction between these two strands of environmentalism is the cause of a split within the scientific community about nuclear energy.\nOn one side are purists who believe nuclear power isn't worth the risk and the exclusive solution to the climate crisis is renewable energy. The opposing side agrees that renewables are crucial, but says society needs an amount of power available to meet consumers' basic demands when the sun isn't shining and the wind isn't blowing. Nuclear energy, being far cleaner than oil, gas and coal, is a natural option, especially where hydroelectric capacity is limited.\nLeon Clarke, who helped author reports for the UN's Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change, isn't an uncritical supporter of nuclear energy, but says it's a valuable option to have if we're serious about reaching carbon neutrality.\n\"Core to all of this is the degree to which you think we can actually meet climate goals with 100% renewables,\" he said. \"If you don't believe we can do it, and you care about the climate, you are forced to think about something like nuclear.\"\nThe achievability of universal 100% renewability is similarly contentious. Cities such as Burlington, Vermont, have been \"100% renewable\" for years. But these cities often have small populations, occasionally still rely on fossil fuel energy and have significant renewable resources at their immediate disposal. Meanwhile, countries that manage to run off renewables typically do so thanks to extraordinary hydroelectric capabilities.\nGermany stands as the best case study for a large, industrialized country pushing into green energy. Chancellor Angela Merkel in 2011 announced Energiewende, an energy transition that would phase out nuclear and coal while phasing in renewables. Wind and solar power generation has increased over 400% since 2010, and renewables provided 46% of the country's electricity in 2019.\nBut progress has halted in recent years. The instability of renewables doesn't just mean energy is often not produced at night, but also that solar and wind can overwhelm the grid during the day, forcing utilities to pay customers to use their electricity. Lagging grid infrastructure struggles to transport this overabundance of green energy from Germany's north to its industrial south, meaning many factories still run on coal and gas. The political limit has also been reached in some places, with citizens meeting the construction of new wind turbines with loud protests.\nThe result is that Germany's greenhouse gas emissions have fallen by around 11.5% since 2010—slower than the EU average of 13.5%."}, "questions": [{"id": "2024-06-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What does the woman say she will do?", "options": ["A) Reply to the man's last proposal within a short time.", "B) Sign the agreement if one small change is made to it.", "C) Make a sponsorship deal for her client at the meeting.", "D) Give the man some good news regarding the contract."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Sign the agreement if one small change is made to it.\n【定位】W: I have, indeed. And I wish to sign the agreement pending one small change to be made to\nthe contract.\n女：我确实同意了。而且我希望签署这份协议，前提是合同里做一处小改动。\n【信号】被问者的直接应答 + I wish to 表态。上一句男方问 Have you agreed to my last\nproposal?，女方紧接着应答 I have, indeed. And I wish to sign the agreement\npending one small change，这句既是被问者的直接回应，又含 I wish to 的明确表态，正\n是答案句。\n【替换】I wish to sign the agreement ↔ Sign the agreement\nI wish to do = 表示要做该动作\npending one small change to be made to the contract ↔ if one small change is made to it\npending = if，都是 \"先改一处为条件\"\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 借了男方话里的 my last proposal 拼成 Reply to the man's last proposal within a\nshort time.，可她在 I have, indeed 里已经回复并表态要签，根本没说 \"短时间内回复\"，\nwithin a short time 是凭空加的。\n借⽤原⽂：Have you agreed to my last proposal?\nC) 张冠李戴\nC 的 Make a sponsorship deal for her client at the meeting. 用了录音里的 make a\nsponsorship deal for your clients，但那是男方反问 \"你到底要不要做这笔交易\"，并非女\n方此刻 \"我要做什么\" 的回答；本题问的是她当下表态，答案是签约。\n借⽤原⽂：Are you sure you want to make a sponsorship deal for your clients\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 的 Give the man some good news regarding the contract. 盗了男方那句 please\ngive me some good news，那是男方的请求 / 期待，女方并没说 \"我要给你好消息\"，她\n说的是愿意签约。\n借⽤原⽂：please give me some good news", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What does the man say about some people he represents?", "options": ["A) They are becoming impatient.", "B) They are afraid time is running out.", "C) They are used to making alterations.", "D) They are concerned about the details."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They are becoming impatient.\n【定位】M: I ask this, because, frankly, some people at my end are running out of patience.\n男：我之所以这么问，是因为坦白说，我这边有些人快没耐心了。\n【信号】因果 because 后给出他对己方人员的真实描述。他用 because, frankly, some people at\nmy end are running out of patience，because 引出对 \"我这边的人\" 的实情描述，\nfrankly（坦白说）更是加重语气的强调信号，这句就是答案句。\n【替换】running out of patience ↔ becoming impatient\nrunning out of patience = impatient，同义改写\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB 的 time is running out 盗用了录音 running out of patience 里的 running out，却把\n宾语偷换成 time。录音里耗尽的是耐心，不是时间，B 在 running out 后面动了手脚。\n借⽤原⽂：running out of patience\nC) 答非所问\nC 的 used to making alterations 取自录音 making alterations，但那是男方说双方 \"这一\n年来回改来改去\" 的背景，不是在描述 \"我方那些人现在什么状态\"，答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：we've been through this for almost a year now, back and forth, making alterations\nD) 张冠李戴\nD 的 concerned about the details 实际对应女方后面说的 We therefore comb through\nthe fine details of all contracts，那是女方一边推敲细节，与男方所说 \"我那边的人没耐\n心\" 是两拨人、两回事。\n借⽤原⽂：We therefore comb through the fine details of all contracts", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What reason does the woman give for the new deal to exclude the Middle East?", "options": ["A) To prevent geographical discrimination.", "B) To tap the food and beverage market.", "C) To avoid any conflict of interest.", "D) To reduce unfair competition."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） To avoid any conflict of interest.\n【定位】W: Therefore, in order to avoid any conflict, we would need to ensure that both marketing\ncampaigns do not overlap geographically.\n女：因此，为了避免任何冲突，我们需要确保两个营销活动在地域上不重叠。\n【信号】in order to / therefore 双因果信号引出真目的。她先讲客户在中东另有营销意向，再用\nTherefore, in order to avoid any conflict, we would need to ensure that both\nmarketing campaigns do not overlap geographically，therefore 与 in order to 双双\n标出这是给理由的句子，正是答案句。\n【替换】in order to avoid any conflict ↔ To avoid any conflict of interest.\navoid any conflict 与选项几乎原义对应\nboth marketing campaigns do not overlap geographically ↔ conflict of interest\n两活动地域不重叠 = 不产生利益冲突\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 的 To prevent geographical discrimination. 蹭了录音 overlap geographically 里的\ngeographically，造出 \"地域歧视\"。原文讲的是两个营销活动地域别重叠，与 \"歧视\" 毫不\n相干，是借词造假。\n借⽤原⽂：both marketing campaigns do not overlap geographically\nB) 答非所问\nB 的 To tap the food and beverage market. 用了录音 our food and beverage\nmarket，但那是男方在第12段说自家所属行业，与女方排除中东的理由无关；她的理由是\n避免冲突，不是去开发某市场。\n借⽤原⽂：So long as the product is very different from our food and beverage market\nD) 未提及\nD 的 To reduce unfair competition. 听着像商务套话，但录音通篇没说要减少不公平竞\n争，她讲的是避免客户形象在同一地域撞车的利益冲突，competition 一词原文压根没出\n现。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What does the man say about the Middle East?", "options": ["A) It is a potential market for food and beverage.", "B) It is very attractive for real estate developers.", "C) It is a negligible market for his company.", "D) It is very different from other markets."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It is a negligible market for his company.\n【定位】M: The Middle East is a negligible market for us, but I still need to check this with a couple\nof departments.\n男：中东对我们来说是个可以忽略的市场，不过我还是得跟几个部门核实一下。\n【信号】他对中东的直接评价句 + 形容词 negligible。他直说 The Middle East is a negligible\nmarket for us，主系表结构直接给出评价形容词 negligible，是对中东的正面定性句，正\n是答案句；后半 but 起的只是补充程序，不改这句评价。\n【替换】a negligible market for us ↔ a negligible market for his company\nfor us = for his company，几乎原义\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nA 的 It is a potential market for food and beverage. 把中东说成 \"有潜力\"，与他原话 a\nnegligible market（可忽略）正好相反；food and beverage 又是蹭了他讲己方行业的熟\n词，双重误导。\n借⽤原⽂：The Middle East is a negligible market for us\nB) 张冠李戴\nB 的 It is very attractive for real estate developers. 借了女方说的中东 Real estate，但\n那是女方客户在中东另有房地产营销意向，并非男方对中东市场的评价，张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：Real estate\nD) 偷换概念\nD 的 It is very different from other markets. 盗用了 the product is very different from\nour food and beverage market 里的 very different，但原文说的是 \"产品\" 与食饮市场不\n同，不是说 \"中东市场\" 与别的市场不同，主语被偷换。\n借⽤原⽂：So long as the product is very different from our food and beverage market", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What does the woman say about all the astronomers at the Denver Observatory?", "options": ["A) They are thrilled by a rare astronomic phenomenon.", "B) They are celebrating a big event on mountain tops.", "C) They are enthusiastic about big science-related stories.", "D) They are joined by astronomers all across North America."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They are thrilled by a rare astronomic phenomenon.\n【定位】Yes, all the astronomers on site here are very excited.\n是的，在这里现场的所有天文学家都非常兴奋。\n【信号】被问者的直接应答。男士在 what is the big story over there? 抛出问题，女士开口第一句\nall the astronomers on site here are very excited 就是直接应答，对话题的答案落在回答\n方，这句即落点。\n【替换】all the astronomers on site here are very excited ↔\nThey are thrilled by a rare astronomic phenomenon.\nexcited 同义改写为 thrilled\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\n录音 congregating on mountain tops 确实念到\"山顶\"，但那是\"聚集去观星\"，不是\ncelebrating a big event on mountain tops 的\"在山顶庆祝\"，且主语是北美各地爱好者，\n非天文台的天文学家。\n借⽤原⽂：congregating on mountain tops tonight to watch the night's sky\nC) 原词陷阱\n开头 a special science-related news story 念到\"科学相关\"，C 项 enthusiastic about\nbig science-related stories 直接搬这串词，但录音说的是天文学家\"为这次天象兴奋\"，不\n是\"热衷各种科学新闻故事\"，把对一次具体天象的兴奋偷换成对科学故事的泛泛热衷。\n借⽤原⽂：a special science-related news story\nD) 张冠李戴\n录音 space enthusiasts all across North America 说的是\"北美各地的爱好者要聚集\"，D\n项 joined by astronomers all across North America 把它改成\"天文台的天文学家被北美\n各地天文学家加入\"，主语和事件都被偷换，原文没有这层意思。\n借⽤原⽂：space enthusiasts all across North America and the rest of the Northern Hemisphere\nwill be congregating", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What do we learn from the conversation about the Oppenheimer comet?", "options": ["A) It will be the most formidable of its kind in over a century.", "B) It will come closest to Earth in more than one hundred years.", "C) It will eclipse many other such events in human history.", "D) It will be seen most clearly from Denver's mountain tops."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It will come closest to Earth in more than one hundred years.\n【定位】Tonight, the Earth will come into close proximity with the Oppenheimer comet. It is the\nclosest our planet has been to such a phenomenon in over 100 years.\n今晚，地球将与奥本海默彗星近距离接近。这是一百多年来我们的星球离这样一种天象最近\n的一次。\n【信号】正面定性句 + 时间数字。女士直接给彗星定性 It is the closest our planet has been to\nsuch a phenomenon in over 100 years，\"closest…in over 100 years\" 把\"距离最\n近\"和\"百年\"绑在一起，是带数字的定性句，正是答案落点。\n【替换】the closest our planet has been to such a phenomenon in over 100 years ↔\nIt will come closest to Earth in more than one hundred years.\n主被动对调 + 数字同义\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音后文 this formidable object 确有 \"formidable\"，A 项 the most formidable of its\nkind in over a century 便把\"百年\"嫁接到\"最厉害\"上；但原文里\"百年\"修饰的是\"距离最\n近\"，从没说它是百年来\"最厉害\"的，是把数字按到了错的形容词上。\n借⽤原⽂：in order to see this formidable object\nC) 过度推断\neclipse many other such events in human history 借\"eclipse\"（此处作动词\"使相形见\n绌\"）和前文 Is there an eclipse happening soon? 的\"日食\"玩文字游戏，原文从没说这颗\n彗星\"使人类历史上其他天象相形见绌\"，属凭空拔高。\n借⽤原⽂：Is there an eclipse happening soon?\nD) 原词陷阱\nseen most clearly from Denver's mountain tops 把前文 the Denver Observatory\n的\"丹佛\"和 mountain tops 的\"山顶\"拼到一起，但原文只说爱好者会聚到山顶观星，从没\n说\"从丹佛的山顶看得最清楚\"，是把零散原词攒成一句没说过的话。\n借⽤原⽂：congregating on mountain tops tonight to watch the night's sky", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What does the woman say people will only see in the sky without a telescope?", "options": ["A) blur.", "B) Stars.", "C) The edge of our galaxy.", "D) An ordinary flying object."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） blur.\n【定位】People will only see a blur without one.\n没有望远镜的话，人们只会看到模糊的一团。\n【信号】条件 without one + only 直陈。这句用 without one（one 指代上文 telescope）锁\n定\"不用望远镜\"的前提，再用 only see a blur 直说看到什么，前提和结果都摆明，是答\n案句。\n【替换】People will only see a blur without one ↔ blur.\n原句直取，前提一致\n【排除】B) 未提及\n录音通篇没说不用望远镜会看到\"星星\"，Stars. 是凭\"看星空\"的常识脑补出来的，原文只说\n看到\"一团模糊\"。\nC) 原词陷阱\non the edge of our galaxy 念到\"星系边缘\"，但那是说彗星\"所在的位置\"，不是\"不用望远\n镜看到的东西\"，C 项 The edge of our galaxy. 把\"它在哪\"偷换成\"看到什么\"。\n借⽤原⽂：The Oppenheimer comet will still be millions of miles away, on the edge of our galaxy\nD) 与原文相反\nwonder at this flying object 念到 \"flying object\"，但那是\"用普通望远镜能观赏到的彗\n星\"，D 项 An ordinary flying object. 把它挪到\"不用望远镜\"的前提下——条件正好反了，\n不用望远镜只见模糊团，看不清是个\"飞行物\"。\n借⽤原⽂：Even the most ordinary of telescopes should be conducive for people to observe and\nwonder at this flying object", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What do scientists at the Denver Observatory advise amateurs do to facilitate their observation?", "options": ["A) Use professional equipment.", "B) Climb to the nearby heights.", "C) Fix their eyes due north.", "D) Make use of phone apps."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Make use of phone apps.\n【定位】And scientists here have told me there are plenty of phone apps that will facilitate this.\n这里的科学家告诉我，有很多手机应用程序可以帮上这个忙。\n【信号】建议句 + 与题干同词 facilitate。女士转述科学家的建议 there are plenty of phone apps\nthat will facilitate this，\"scientists…told me\"对应题干的\"scientists\nadvise\"，\"facilitate\"与题干 facilitate their observation 同词，是答案句。\n【替换】there are plenty of phone apps that will facilitate this ↔ Make use of phone apps.\n存在句 → 祈使建议\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n录音 that does not mean one needs professional equipment 明确说\"并不需要专业设\n备\"，A 项 Use professional equipment. 直接搬这串原词却把否定改成肯定，意思正好反\n了。\n借⽤原⽂：that does not mean one needs professional equipment\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音 congregating on mountain tops 念到\"山顶\"，B 项 Climb to the nearby heights.\n（爬到附近高处）借此设套，但那是说爱好者去哪儿聚集，不是科学家\"为方便观测给的建\n议\"，问的根本不是同一件事。\n借⽤原⽂：will be congregating on mountain tops tonight to watch the night's sky\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音 The comet will be almost exactly due north 念到\"正北\"，那是在说彗星\"在天上的\n位置\"，C 项 Fix their eyes due north. 把\"它在正北方\"偷换成\"建议人们眼睛盯着正北\"，而\n科学家给的方便观测的办法是用 app，不是\"盯着北方\"。\n借⽤原⽂：The comet will be almost exactly due north, at 60 degrees above the equator", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What question is said to have recently stirred debate?", "options": ["A) Whether consumers should be warned against ultra-processed foods.", "B) Whether there is sufficient scientific consensus on dietary guidelines.", "C) Whether guidelines can form the basis for nutrition advice to consumers.", "D) Whether food scientists will agree on the concept of ultra-processed foods."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Whether consumers should be warned against ultra-processed foods.\n【定位】While there is strong scientific consensus around most existing guidelines, one question has\nrecently stirred debate: should consumers be warned to avoid ultra-processed foods?\n尽管围绕现有的大多数指南存在强有力的科学共识，但近来有一个问题引发了争论：是否应\n当警告消费者避免食用超加工食品？\n【信号】冒号后正面点出争论问题。信号是 one question has recently stirred debate: 这一句——\n它直接照应题干 stirred debate，而冒号之后的 should consumers be warned to avoid\nultra-processed foods? 才是被点名的那个争论问题，冒号在这里起「即将给出答案」的提\n示作用。\n【替换】should consumers be warned to avoid ultra-processed foods? ↔\nWhether consumers should be warned against ultra-processed foods.\n疑问句→Whether名词从句，warned against=warned to avoid\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB Whether there is sufficient scientific consensus on dietary guidelines. 借用了录音里\nstrong scientific consensus 这个原词，但原文说的是「围绕大多数指南已存在强共识」，\n共识是既定背景、不是争论的问题；真正起争论的是冒号后那句，B 答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：While there is strong scientific consensus around most existing guidelines\nC) 原词陷阱\nC Whether guidelines can form the basis for nutrition advice to consumers. 几乎照搬\n开篇 Dietary guidelines form the basis for nutrition advice，但原文这句是肯定的陈述\n（指南本就是营养建议的基础），并非被拿来争论的疑问，C 把陈述句伪装成争论点。\n借⽤原⽂：Dietary guidelines form the basis for nutrition advice and regulations around the world.\nD) 未提及\nD Whether food scientists will agree on the concept of ultra-processed foods. 用了\n话题词 ultra-processed foods，但「食品科学家是否会就这一概念达成一致」原文根本没\n说——录音争论的是「要不要警告消费者」，不是「科学家认不认这个概念」，属无中生\n有。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "How does the system developed by Monteiro and colleagues classify foods?", "options": ["A) By the labor cost for the final products.", "B) By the degree of industrial processing.", "C) By the extent of chemical alteration.", "D) By the convention of classification."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） By the degree of industrial processing.\n【定位】The debate centers around a system developed by Monteiro and colleagues that classifies\nfoods by their degree of industrial processing, ranging from unprocessed to ultra-processed.\n这场争论围绕蒙泰罗及其同事开发的一套系统展开，该系统按食物的工业加工程度对其分\n类，从未加工到超加工。\n【信号】专名+classifies…by 直接给出分类标准。信号是题干专名 Monteiro 在录音里复现，紧跟\n的 classifies foods by their degree of industrial processing 用 classify…by… 句式正面\n给出分类依据，这句就是答案句。\n【替换】by their degree of industrial processing ↔ By the degree of industrial processing.\n近乎原话对应，仅去掉物主代词\n【排除】C) 原词陷阱\nC By the extent of chemical alteration. 来自后一句 alter them with chemicals——但那\n句是在「定义什么叫超加工食品」，分类的标准是「工业加工程度」，化学改变只是超加工\n这一端的特征描述，不是整套系统的分类轴，C 答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：alter them with chemicals in order to formulate the final product\nA) 未提及\nA By the labor cost for the final products. 里 final products 蹭了原文 formulate the\nfinal product 的字眼，但「按人工成本分类」录音从没说过；原文提到的是 cheap（便\n宜），与「人工成本」不是一回事，属无中生有。\nD) 与原文相反\nD By the convention of classification. 借了 food classification 里的 classification 一\n词，但原文恰恰强调这套系统是「超越」传统分类的——第2段说要 beyond conventional\nfood classification systems，方向正好相反，D 取了被否定的那一端。\n借⽤原⽂：beyond conventional food classification systems", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What is consumption of ultra-processed foods linked with, according to studies?", "options": ["A) Increased consumers' expenses.", "B) Greater risk of chronic diseases.", "C) People's misunderstanding of nutrition.", "D) Children's dislike for unprocessed foods."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Greater risk of chronic diseases.\n【定位】Studies have linked consumption of ultra-processed foods—which are packed high in salt,\nsugar, and fat—with weight gain and an increased risk of chronic diseases, even after\nadjusting for the amount of salt, sugar, and fat in the diet.\n多项研究已将食用超加工食品（这类食品盐、糖、脂肪含量极高）与体重增加和慢性病风险\n升高联系在一起，即便在调整了膳食中盐、糖、脂肪的摄入量之后仍是如此。\n【信号】Studies have linked…with 正面给出关联后果。信号是 Studies have linked\nconsumption of ultra-processed foods…with weight gain and an increased risk of\nchronic diseases，与题干 linked with…according to studies 措辞高度对应，linked…\nwith 后面的内容就是答案；注意中间被 salt/sugar/fat 的插入语隔开，要听完整句。\n【替换】an increased risk of chronic diseases ↔ Greater risk of chronic diseases.\nincreased=greater，risk of chronic diseases 同义保留\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA Increased consumers' expenses. 蹭了第3段超加工食品 designed to be cheap 的语境\n联想，但原文说这类食品「便宜」，恰恰不是「让消费者花费增加」；研究关联的是健康后\n果，不是花钱，A 属无中生有。\nC) 未提及\nC People's misunderstanding of nutrition. 借了全文反复出现的 nutrition 话题词，但\n「人们对营养的误解」录音从没说过；研究关联的是体重和疾病，不是认知误解，C 无中生\n有。\nD) 未提及\nD Children's dislike for unprocessed foods. 用了第3段 unprocessed 一词，但「孩子不\n喜欢未加工食品」录音完全没提，且把话题从「健康后果」偷换成「儿童口味偏好」，属无\n中生有。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What do psychologists say people do when they are short of resources?", "options": ["A) They begin to think of the benefits of constraints.", "B) They try to seek solutions from creative people.", "C) They try hard to maximize their mental energy.", "D) They begin to see the world in a different way."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They begin to see the world in a different way.\n【定位】According to psychologists, when you have less to work with, you actually begin to see the\nworld differently.\n据心理学家所说，当你可支配的东西更少时，你实际上会开始用不同的方式看待世界。\n【信号】题干信号词 psychologists 顺听定位。题干问的是 psychologists（心理学家）的说法，录\n音里恰有 According to psychologists 这一引述信号，紧跟其后的 you actually begin to\nsee the world differently 就是答案句；篇章题题目多与录音同序，听到 psychologists 就\n知道答案落在它后面那句。\n【替换】begin to see the world differently ↔ begin to see the world in a different way\n副词换介词短语\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 用了全文话题词 constraints，但 think of the benefits of constraints（想到约束的好\n处）是录音从未说过的动作，录音说的是人开始换个方式看世界，A 只是借 constraints 这\n个耳熟的词诱你误选。\n借⽤原⽂：human creativity benefits from constraints\nB) 未提及\nB 说人去 seek solutions from creative people（向有创造力的人求解），录音通篇讲的是\n人自己换方式看世界、自己更灵活地用资源，从没说去向别人求助，属无中生有。\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 出现 maximize their mental energy，录音确有 dedicate your mental energy（投入\n心智能量）这个词，但原文说的是把心智能量投入到更灵活地行动，不是 maximize（最大\n化）能量本身，C 盗用 mental energy 这个原词却篡改了它的动作。\n借⽤原⽂：you dedicate your mental energy to acting more resourcefully", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What does the passage say about innovation?", "options": ["A) It is characteristic of all creative people.", "B) It is essential to pushing society forward.", "C) It is a creative person's response to limitation.", "D) It is an impetus to socio-economic development."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It is a creative person's response to limitation.\n【定位】Innovation is a creative person's response to limitation.\n创新是有创造力的人对限制做出的回应。\n【信号】段首定性句直接定义。第三段开头 Innovation is a creative person's response to\nlimitation. 是一句标准的 X is Y 定性句，主语正是题干的 innovation；篇章题里这种段首\n主语直接成句的定义往往就是答案句，听到 Innovation is 就该锁定。\n【替换】a creative person's response to limitation ↔ a creative person's response to limitation\n罕见的近乎原句对应\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nA 说创新 characteristic of all creative people（所有有创造力的人的特征），录音说的是\n创新是有创造力者对限制的回应，并没有断言它是所有这类人都具备的特征，all 把范围说\n死，属偷换概念。\n借⽤原⽂：The most successful creative people know that constraints give their minds the impetus\nto leap higher.\nB) 过度推断\nB 说创新 essential to pushing society forward（对推动社会前进至关重要），录音有\nimpetus（推动力）一词，但说的是约束给人的头脑以跃升的推动力，并非创新对推动社会\n前进必不可少，B 把对象偷偷拔高到社会层面，过度推断。\n借⽤原⽂：constraints give their minds the impetus to leap higher\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 说创新是 impetus to socio-economic development（社会经济发展的推动力），\nimpetus 虽是原词，但 socio-economic development（社会经济发展）录音从头到尾没\n提，纯属拿原词拼接出一个原文没有的结论。\n借⽤原⽂：constraints give their minds the impetus to leap higher", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What did a 2015 study by Ravi Mehta and Meng Zhu find?", "options": ["A) Scarcity or abundance of resources has little impact on people's creativity.", "B) Innovative people are not constrained in connecting unrelated concepts.", "C) People have no incentive to use available resources in new ways.", "D) Creative people tend to consume more available resources."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） People have no incentive to use available resources in new ways.\n【定位】Ravi Mehta at the University of Illinois and Meng Zhu at Johns Hopkins University found\nthat people simply have no incentive to use what's available to them in novel ways.\n伊利诺伊大学的拉维·梅塔和约翰·霍普金斯大学的朱萌发现，人们根本没有动力以新颖的方\n式使用手头现有的东西。\n【信号】人名加 found that 引出发现。录音里 Ravi Mehta 与 Meng Zhu 两个人名后紧跟 found\nthat，这是研究发现的标志信号，that 引导的从句就是答案内容；听到人名加 found\nthat，答案句必在其后。\n【替换】no incentive to use what's available to them in novel ways ↔\nno incentive to use available resources in new ways\n近义词逐处替换\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nA 说 Scarcity or abundance of resources has little impact on people's creativity（资源\n稀缺或充裕对创造力影响不大），录音确实研究了 scarcity or abundance 如何影响创造性\n用资源，但结论恰恰是影响很大、稀缺反而激发创造，A 把研究的对象词盗来却得出与原文\n相反的结论。\n借⽤原⽂：how thinking about scarcity or abundance influences how creatively people use\ntheir resources\nB) 答非所问\nB 说 Innovative people are not constrained in connecting unrelated concepts（创新\n者在联结不相关概念时不受约束），录音末句确有 connecting unrelated ideas and\nconcepts 这个搭配，但那是说持续的约束帮助人更善于联结不相关概念，并非这项 2015\n研究的发现，B 把别处的原词搬到研究结论上，答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：Consistent constraints help you improve at connecting unrelated ideas and concepts.\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 说 Creative people tend to consume more available resources（有创造力的人倾向\n消耗更多现有资源），available resources 蹭了原文的词，但 consume more（消耗更\n多）录音从未提及，录音讲的是以新方式使用资源，不是消耗更多，纯属编造。\n借⽤原⽂：use what's available to them in novel ways", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What did Marissa Mayer once write concerning creativity?", "options": ["A) It is key to a company's survival.", "B) It shapes and focuses problems.", "C) It is essential to meeting challenges", "D) It thrives best when constrained."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It thrives best when constrained.\n【定位】Constraints shape and focus problems and provide clear challenges to overcome; Creativity\nthrives best when constrained.\n约束塑造并聚焦问题，并提供明确的、需要去克服的挑战；创造力在受约束时最为繁盛。\n【信号】题干限定词 creativity 锁后半句。Mayer 的引述有前后两句，前半 Constraints shape\nand focus problems 主语是约束，后半 Creativity thrives best when constrained 主语\n才是 creativity；题干限定 concerning creativity，信号就是这个限定词，答案锁后半句。\n【替换】Creativity thrives best when constrained ↔ It thrives best when constrained.\n代词 It 回指 creativity\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 说创造力 key to a company's survival（对公司生存至关重要），虽然录音提到 Mayer\n曾是 Google 的副总裁，但她写的话里根本没提公司生存，A 凭 Google 这一职务背景臆造\n出公司话题，属未提及。\nB) 张冠李戴\nB 说 It shapes and focuses problems.，这句确实摘自 Mayer 的话 Constraints shape\nand focus problems，但原文该动作的主语是 Constraints（约束）不是 creativity，题干\n问的是关于 creativity 的内容，B 把约束做的事安到了创造力头上，主语张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：Constraints shape and focus problems\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 说创造力 essential to meeting challenges（对应对挑战至关重要），录音里\nchallenges 一词出自 provide clear challenges to overcome，说的是约束提供需克服的\n挑战，而非创造力对应对挑战必不可少，C 盗用 challenges 偷换了逻辑关系。\n借⽤原⽂：provide clear challenges to overcome", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "Why does the speaker say strategies for managing conflicts can always be changed?", "options": ["A) Because they are learned.", "B) Because they come naturally.", "C) Because they have to be properly personalized.", "D) Because there can be more effective strategies."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Because they are learned.\n【定位】But we do have a personal strategy, and because it is learned, we can always change it by\nlearning new and more effective ways of managing conflicts.\n但我们确实有一套个人策略，而正因为它是后天习得的，我们总能通过学习新的、更有效的\n冲突处理方式来改变它。\n【信号】because 直接交代原因。这句里 because it is learned, we can always change it 是赤裸\n裸的因果结构：\"because + 原因\"紧跟着\"can always change\"。原因题听到 because 就锁\n这句，不必再听后文。\n【替换】because it is learned ↔ Because they are learned.\n几乎原句搬到选项\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音确实说过 We just do whatever seems to come naturally，但那是讲\"我们平时不自\n觉、顺其自然地行事\"，并非\"策略能被改变的原因\"。B 把这个原词搬来当原因，恰恰用反了\n——天生自然反而是难改的那一面。\n借⽤原⽂：We just do whatever seems to come naturally\nC) 偷换概念\n录音提到策略是 a personal strategy（个人的），C 顺势造出 they have to be properly\npersonalized（必须妥善个性化）。但录音从没说\"必须被个性化\"是它能改变的原因，这是\n把\"个人的\"偷换成\"必须个性化\"的无中生有。\n借⽤原⽂：a personal strategy\nD) 答非所问\n录音末尾确有 more effective ways，D 据此说 there can be more effective strategies。\n但\"有更有效的策略\"在录音里是\"改变之后能达到的效果/途径\"，不是\"能改变\"的原因；真正\n的原因是它习得（learned）。D 把结果当成了原因。\n借⽤原⽂：more effective ways of managing conflicts", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What is said to affect the way one acts in a conflict?", "options": ["A) The extent of difference and of similarity between the two sides.", "B) The knowledge of the specific expectation the other side holds.", "C) The importance of one's goals and of the relationship.", "D) The approaches one adopts to conflict management."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） The importance of one's goals and of the relationship.\n【定位】How important your personal goals are and how important the relationship is to you affect\nhow you act in a conflict.\n你的个人目标有多重要、以及这段关系对你有多重要，这两点影响你在冲突中如何行事。\n【信号】原句直接出现 affect。这句的主干就是 How important your personal goals are and\nhow important the relationship is to you affect how you act，动词 affect 与题干\naffect 同词出现，谓语前的两个主语（目标重要性、关系重要性）即所求，是教科书式\n的\"信号词同词复现\"定位点。\n【替换】How important your personal goals are and how important the relationship is to you ↔\nThe importance of one's goals and of the relationship.\n句子改名词化\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 说 The extent of difference and of similarity between the two sides.（双方差异与相\n似的程度）听上去很学术，但录音通篇没说\"差异/相似程度\"会影响行为。这是凭\"冲突=双\n方有分歧\"的常识脑补出来的，原文无据。\nB) 原词陷阱\nB 说 The knowledge of the specific expectation the other side holds.（了解对方的具\n体期待）。录音确实反复说到\"the other person\"，但从没说\"知道对方期待什么\"会影响你\n的行为，影响行为的是你自己目标和关系的重要性。B 借了\"对方\"这个原词，却安了个录音\n没说的内容。\n借⽤原⽂：keeping a good relationship with the other person\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 说 The approaches one adopts to conflict management.（处理冲突所采取的方\n法）。这正是整篇的大主题词 managing conflicts，但题目问的是\"什么影响你的做法\"，\n而\"做法本身\"不能影响\"做法\"，逻辑上自指。D 盗用了贯穿全文的话题词来冒充答案。\n借⽤原⽂：strategies for managing conflicts", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "Of the five styles the speaker discusses, which views conflicts as problems to be solved?", "options": ["A) The fox.", "B) The owl.", "C) The shark.", "D) The turtle."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） The owl.\n【定位】The owl. Owls view conflicts as problems to be solved.\n猫头鹰。猫头鹰把冲突视为有待解决的问题。\n【信号】原句逐字复述特征。第 6 段开头先报名字 The owl，紧接着 Owls view conflicts as\nproblems to be solved 与题干 views conflicts as problems to be solved 几乎一字不\n差。匹配题里，题干特征在哪段被原样念出，那段的主角就是答案。\n【替换】Owls view conflicts as problems to be solved ↔ The owl.\n特征绑定到名字\n【排除】A) 答非所问\nA 是 The fox.。狐狸的特征是 both sides gain something（双方各让一步、各得一点），\n属于折中妥协，录音没说狐狸\"把冲突看作待解决的问题\"。它和 owl 都偏正面，最易混，但\n定性不同。\n借⽤原⽂：They seek a solution to conflicts where both sides gain something.\nC) 与原文相反\nC 是 The shark.。鲨鱼的特征是 overpower opponents by forcing them to accept their\nsolution（强压对方、逼其接受），与\"把冲突当问题来解决\"正相反。鲨鱼追求一方赢一方\n输，绝非协商解决。\n借⽤原⽂：Sharks try to overpower opponents by forcing them to accept their solution\nD) 与原文相反\nD 是 The turtle.。乌龟的特征是 withdraw into their shells to avoid conflicts（缩进壳里\n逃避冲突），它连面对都不愿意，更谈不上\"把冲突当问题来解决\"，与题干特征截然相反。\n借⽤原⽂：Turtles withdraw into their shells to avoid conflicts.", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What do scientists hope to do by cracking the genetic code of plants and animals?", "options": ["A) Help save species from extinction and boost human health.", "B) Understand how plants and animals perished over the past.", "C) Help gather information publicly available to researchers.", "D) Find out the cause of extinction of Britain's 66, 000 species."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Help save species from extinction and boost human health.\n【定位】The genetic code of all 1.5 million known species of animals and plants living on earth will\nbe mapped to help save species from extinction and boost human health.\n地球上全部 150 万已知动植物物种的基因密码都将被绘制出来，以帮助拯救物种免于灭绝、\n并提升人类健康。\n【信号】目的信号 to help 后面接落点。答案句靠目的状语 to help save species from extinction\nand boost human health 中的 to help 锁定——\"做基因测序\"的目的正写在 to help 之\n后，这正是题干 hope to do 要的那一层。\n【替换】save species from extinction and boost human health ↔\nHelp save species from extinction and boost human health.\n选项几乎原句搬来，只在前面加 Help\n【排除】B) 答非所问\n本项 Understand how plants and animals perished over the past. 借了\"动植物消亡\"的\n料（呼应录音第 3 段大量物种灭绝的描述），但说的是\"理解过去它们如何消亡\"，而录音目\n的是\"拯救现在的物种\"，把\"救（未来）\"偷换成\"懂（过去）\"，答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：has already led to the loss of at least 77 species of mammals and 140 types of birds\nsince 1500\nC) 原词陷阱\nHelp gather information publicly available to researchers. 直接搬了录音 all the\ninformation will be publicly available to researchers，但那是说\"项目完成后信息将公开\n给研究者\"，是项目的副产品，不是\"破解基因码\"这一动作的目的，典型原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：all the information will be publicly available to researchers\nD) 原词陷阱\nFind out the cause of extinction of Britain's 66, 000 species. 搬了录音 Britain's 66,000\nspecies 这个醒目数字，又拼上\"找灭绝原因\"，但录音从没说目的是\"查灭绝原因\"，且\n66,000 是英国测序的物种数、与\"破解基因码的目的\"是两码事，原词＋无中生有。\n借⽤原⽂：Britain's 66,000 species of animals and plants", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What do many scientists believe with regard to earth?", "options": ["A) It was once dominated by dinosaurs.", "B) It has entered the sixth mass extinction.", "C) Its prospects depend on future human behaviour.", "D) Its climate change is aggravated by humans."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It has entered the sixth mass extinction.\n【定位】Many scientists believe that earth has now entered the sixth mass extinction, with humans\ncreating a toxic mix of habitat loss, pollution and climate change.\n许多科学家相信，地球如今已进入第六次大规模物种灭绝，人类制造出栖息地丧失、污染和\n气候变化交织而成的有毒组合。\n【信号】观点信号 Many scientists believe 直接引出。答案句由 Many scientists believe that\nearth has now entered the sixth mass extinction 这个观点引导句锁定——题干 believe\n与原文 believe 对齐，believe 后的 that 从句就是\"许多科学家相信什么\"的答案。\n【替换】earth has now entered the sixth mass extinction ↔ It has entered the sixth mass extinction.\nearth→It，其余原句保留\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nIt was once dominated by dinosaurs. 蹭录音里的 dinosaurs，但原文说的是 the\nbiggest loss of species since the dinosaurs were wiped out 66 million years ago——\n恐龙是被灭绝来做\"损失之大\"的参照，录音从没说\"地球曾被恐龙统治\"，纯属听到\ndinosaurs 就想选的陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：since the dinosaurs were wiped out 66 million years ago\nC) 偷换概念\nIts prospects depend on future human behaviour. 说\"地球前景取决于未来人类行为\"，\n录音第 4 段虽提到 a window on the future，但那是讲项目能模型化未来，并非\"许多科学\n家相信地球前景取决于人类未来行为\"，把别处的零碎拼成新断言，无中生有。\n借⽤原⽂：a window on the past gives you a prospective model on the future\nD) 原词陷阱\nIts climate change is aggravated by humans. 抓住录音 habitat loss, pollution and\nclimate change 里的 climate change 单独放大，但原文是\"栖息地丧失、污染、气候变\n化\"三者并列、共同构成有毒组合，单拎一项当作\"科学家相信地球如何\"，以偏概全。\n借⽤原⽂：a toxic mix of habitat loss, pollution and climate change", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "How does Sir Jim Smith, Director of Science at Wellcome, describe the Darwin Tree of Life Project?", "options": ["A) It dwarfs all other efforts to conserve, protect and restore biodiversity on earth.", "B) It is costly to get started and requires the joint efforts of thousands of scientists.", "C) It can help to bring back the large numbers of plants and animals that have gone extinct.", "D) It is the most exciting, most relevant, most timely and most internationally inspirational."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （态度观点题）\nIt is the most exciting, most relevant, most timely and most internationally inspirational.\n【定位】Sir Jim Smith, Director of Science at Wellcome, said, \"Try as I might, I can't think of a more\nexciting, more relevant, more timely or more internationally inspirational project.\"\nWellcome 科学总监 Jim Smith 爵士说：\"无论我怎么想，都想不出还有比这更激动人心、\n更切题、更及时、更具国际启发性的项目。\"\n【信号】评价语气：I can't think of a more… 是变相最高级。答案句靠 I can't think of a more\nexciting, more relevant, more timely or more internationally inspirational project 这\n个\"想不出更…的\"否定结构锁定——\"想不出比它更…的\"等于\"它是最…的\"，是说话人对项目\n的最高评价。\n【替换】I can't think of a more exciting, more relevant, more timely or more internationally inspirational\nproject\n↔ It is the most exciting, most relevant, most timely and most internationally inspirational.\n\"想不出更…的\"=\"最…的\"，比较级转最高级\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nIt dwarfs all other efforts to conserve, protect and restore biodiversity on earth. 蹭了\n录音 conserve as well as protect and restore biodiversity 的原词，但说\"它使其它一切\n保护努力相形见绌\"是录音没有的比较，且这是 Jim Smith 的评价题，A 拼的是别处第 3 段\n的料，张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：bolster efforts to conserve as well as protect and restore biodiversity\nB) 答非所问\nIt is costly to get started and requires the joint efforts of thousands of scientists. 蹭\n了录音 cost £100 million 与众多科学家协作的料，但这是讲项目的成本与规模，是客观事\n实，不是 Jim Smith\"如何形容/评价\"项目，答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：it is expected to take 10 years and cost £100 million\nC) 过度推断\nIt can help to bring back the large numbers of plants and animals that have gone\nextinct. 用了录音常出现的\"动植物灭绝\"原词，但录音只说项目能助保护、记录生命，从没\n说能\"把已灭绝物种带回来\"，明显过度推断。\n借⽤原⽂：we desperately need to catalogue life on our fragile planet now", "points": ["听力·讲座", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What is indeed just an illusion according to Professor Tom Oliver?", "options": ["A) Cultural identity.", "B) Social evolution.", "C) The Copernican revolution.", "D) Human individuality."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Human individuality.\n【定位】Now, a British academic has claimed that human individuality is indeed just an illusion,\nbecause societies are far more interconnected at a mental, physical, and cultural level than\npeople realise.\n如今，一位英国学者声称，人的个体独立性其实只是一种幻觉，因为社会在心理、生理和文\n化层面上的相互联系远比人们意识到的紧密。\n【信号】原句直陈定性。答案句直接用 human individuality is indeed just an illusion 把某概念点\n定为\"幻觉\"，is just an illusion 这种判定句就是信号，听到\"X 其实只是个幻觉\"就锁定 X。\n【替换】human individuality ↔ Human individuality.\n原词直接对应\n【排除】C) 原词陷阱\nThe Copernican revolution. 盗用了录音第二段反复出现的 Copernican，但哥白尼革命是\n讲者用来作类比的\"参照\"，不是被称为幻觉的对象，属听到 Copernican 就想选的原词陷\n阱。\n借⽤原⽂：the so-called Copernican Revolution\nB) 原词陷阱\nSocial evolution. 借了录音 the cultural evolution of human minds 里的 evolution 一\n词，但\"社会／文化演化\"是讲者描述心智发展的背景，并未被定性为幻觉。\n借⽤原⽂：A significant milestone in the cultural evolution of human minds\nA) 偷换概念\nCultural identity. 与录音 cultural level 的 cultural 沾边，但讲者说的是\"文化层面的联\n系\"，从未把\"文化身份\"称为幻觉，属偷换概念。\n借⽤原⽂：at a mental, physical, and cultural level", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What does Professor Tom Oliver think of the idea that we exist as independent selves at the centre of a subjective universe?", "options": ["A) It is a delusion to be disposed of.", "B) It is prevalent even among academics.", "C) It is a myth spread by John Donne's poem.", "D) It is rooted in the mindset of the 17th century."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度观点题） It is a delusion to be disposed of.\n【定位】However, we have one more big myth to dispose of: that we exist as independent selves at the\ncentre of a subjective universe.\n然而，我们还有一个大迷思需要破除：即我们以为自己是独立的自我、处在一个主观宇宙的\n中心。\n【信号】转折However后是真意。句首 However 转折之后才是讲者真正要表达的态度，他把那个想\n法称为 one more big myth to dispose of（又一个要破除的迷思），处置动词 dispose of\n直接给出否定态度。\n【替换】one more big myth to dispose of ↔ a delusion to be disposed of\nmyth=delusion同义替换\n【排除】D) 原词陷阱\nrooted in the mindset of the 17th century 盗用了录音开头 wrote in the 17th century\n的时间词，但 17 世纪说的是 John Donne 写诗的年代，讲者从未说那个\"独立自我\"的想法\n植根于 17 世纪心态，属原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：wrote in the 17th century\nC) 张冠李戴\na myth spread by John Donne's poem 把\"迷思\"归因到 John Donne 的诗，盗用了开头\n出现的 John Donne，但讲者引 Donne 的诗恰恰是支持\"人互相连接\"，并非说这首诗散布\n了迷思，属张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：every man is a piece of the continent, a part of the main\nB) 未提及\nprevalent even among academics（连学者中都很普遍）原文从未提及，讲者只说这是普\n遍的 illusion，没有专门强调\"在学者中尤其普遍\"，属未提及。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "Why does Professor Tom Oliver claim that the material \"us\" is constantly changing?", "options": ["A) He believes in Copernican philosophical doctrines about the universe.", "B) He has gained ample scientific evidence at the University of Reading.", "C) He has found that our inner self and material self are interconnected.", "D) He contends most of our body cells can only live a few days or weeks."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） He contends most of our body cells can only live a few days or weeks.\n【定位】Professor Oliver argues there are around 37 trillion cells in the body but most have a lifespan\nof just a few days or weeks, so the material \"us\" is constantly changing.\n奥利弗教授指出，人体内约有37万亿个细胞，但大多数细胞的寿命只有几天或几周，因此物\n质意义上的\"我们\"在不断变化。\n【信号】因果so前是原因。句中 so the material \"us\" is constantly changing 的 so 是结果标志，\nso 之前的 most have a lifespan of just a few days or weeks 就是讲者给出的原因，正是\nWhy 题要找的依据句。\n【替换】most have a lifespan of just a few days or weeks ↔\nmost of our body cells can only live a few days or weeks\nlifespan改写为can only live\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nample scientific evidence at the University of Reading 盗用了录音第一段介绍学者时的\nthe University of Reading，但那只是说明他的任职单位，原文从未说他在该校获得了大量\n科学证据，属原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：Professor Tom Oliver, a researcher in the Ecology and Evolution group at the\nUniversity of Reading\nA) 答非所问\nHe believes in Copernican philosophical doctrines about the universe. 又借\nCopernican 一词，但本题问的是\"物质之我为何在变\"，答案是细胞寿命，与哥白尼无关，\n属答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：Just as Copernicus realised the Earth is not the centre of the universe\nC) 偷换概念\nHe has found that our inner self and material self are interconnected. 听上去与全\n篇\"互相连接\"主旨呼应，但原文并未以\"内在自我与物质自我相连\"来解释物质之我为何变\n化，是偷换概念、答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：We are intimately connected to the world around us.", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "How can we solve pressing environmental and societal problems according to Professor Tom Oliver?", "options": ["A) By coming to see how disruptive such problems have got to be.", "B) By realising that we all can do our own bit in such endeavours.", "C) By becoming aware that we are part of a bigger world.", "D) By making joint efforts resolutely and persistently."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） By becoming aware that we are part of a bigger world.\n【定位】Professor Oliver claims that individualism is actually bad for society, and only by realising\nwe are part of a bigger entity can we solve pressing environmental and societal problems.\n奥利弗教授声称，个人主义其实对社会有害，只有意识到我们是更大整体的一部分，我们才\n能解决紧迫的环境与社会问题。\n【信号】only by引出唯一途径。句中 only by realising we are part of a bigger entity 用 only by\n引出唯一解决途径，only by 这种限定条件结构是答案信号，听到\"只有……才能解决……\"就\n锁定 by 后面的办法。\n【替换】realising we are part of a bigger entity ↔ becoming aware that we are part of a bigger world\nentity改写为world\n【排除】D) 未提及\nBy making joint efforts resolutely and persistently（坚定持久地共同努力）听上去正确\n且积极，但原文给的办法是\"意识到自己属于更大整体\"，并未说靠\"坚定持久的共同努力\"，\n属未提及的正确话陷阱。\nB) 偷换概念\nBy realising that we all can do our own bit in such endeavours（意识到人人能尽一份\n力）借了 realising 一词，但原文 realising 后接的是\"我们是更大整体的一部分\"，不是\"人\n人能尽一份力\"，属偷换概念。\n借⽤原⽂：only by realising we are part of a bigger entity\nA) 未提及\nBy coming to see how disruptive such problems have got to be（认清这些问题有多具\n破坏性）原文并未把\"看清问题严重\"作为解决之道，讲者给的解法是意识到自己属于更大整\n体，属未提及。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alleviate", "B) clarity", "C) cognitive", "D) composers", "E) hurt", "F) inhibiting", "G) interrogation", "H) intrinsically", "I) loose", "J) majestic", "K) mandatory", "L) recover", "M) significantly", "N) soothing", "O) wards"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) alleviate\n动词\n【词性槽】be looking to + 动词原形 + stress\n【依据】alleviate stress=缓解压力，是固定搭配\nIf you're looking to __(26)__ stress, you might want to give classical music a try. 与开\n篇 Studies have shown that music can reduce stress and anxiety before surgeries 中\n的 reduce stress 同义改写，alleviate stress 在英语里就是「减轻压力」的标准说法。\n【竞争词】L) recover 搭配错位：通常 recover from stress，不直接 recover stress\nrecover 表「从……恢复」时几乎都用 recover from sth，直接接 stress 不合英语习惯；且本句\n要表达的是「减轻」而非「恢复」压力。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·be looking to + 动词原形"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alleviate", "B) clarity", "C) cognitive", "D) composers", "E) hurt", "F) inhibiting", "G) interrogation", "H) intrinsically", "I) loose", "J) majestic", "K) mandatory", "L) recover", "M) significantly", "N) soothing", "O) wards"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) loose\n动词\n【词性槽】letting + 空 + 名词短语（pleasure-inducing dopamine）\n【依据】loose dopamine=释放多巴胺\nletting __(27)__ pleasure-inducing dopamine(多巴胺) in the brain，loose 这里作动词\n意为「释放」，与「大脑释放令人愉悦的多巴胺」语义完美对应，也呼应 produce a\ncalming effect 的因果链。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 F) —— 看到 letting 容易误以为后跟 -ing 并列。考生可能把\nletting __(27)__ pleasure-inducing dopamine(多巴胺) in the brain 误读成两个 -ing 并\n列，从而填 inhibiting；但 let + 宾语 + 动词原形才是正解，且 inhibiting 与「释放多巴\n胺」语义相反，是典型反向干扰。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·letting + 空 + 名词短语（p"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alleviate", "B) clarity", "C) cognitive", "D) composers", "E) hurt", "F) inhibiting", "G) interrogation", "H) intrinsically", "I) loose", "J) majestic", "K) mandatory", "L) recover", "M) significantly", "N) soothing", "O) wards"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) clarity\n名词\n【词性槽】lead to + 空 + in a person's thinking\n【依据】clarity in thinking=思维清晰\nIt turns out a pleasant mood can lead to __(28)__ in a person's thinking. 中 clarity in\n(one's) thinking 是英语固定表达，意思是「思路清晰」，与「愉悦情绪带来思维清晰」的\n因果链贴合，也承接前文古典音乐的认知促进作用。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·lead to + 空 + in a p"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alleviate", "B) clarity", "C) cognitive", "D) composers", "E) hurt", "F) inhibiting", "G) interrogation", "H) intrinsically", "I) loose", "J) majestic", "K) mandatory", "L) recover", "M) significantly", "N) soothing", "O) wards"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) composers\n名词\n【词性槽】many great + 空 + of classical music + 人名举例\n【依据】三位人名=三位作曲家的上义词\nlike Bach, Beethoven and Handel 是教科书级的古典作曲家清单，composers 与之构成\n「上义词—举例」的关系，语义和复数形态都完美对上。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·many great + 空 + of"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alleviate", "B) clarity", "C) cognitive", "D) composers", "E) hurt", "F) inhibiting", "G) interrogation", "H) intrinsically", "I) loose", "J) majestic", "K) mandatory", "L) recover", "M) significantly", "N) soothing", "O) wards"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) cognitive\n形容词\n【词性槽】improve + 空 + function（名词）\n【依据】cognitive function=认知功能，脑科学固定搭配\nlistening to Mozart can increase brain wave activity and improve __(30)__ function.\n与前文 brain wave activity、以及后文 high-level mental functions 同主题串联，\ncognitive function 是脑科学英语中最常见的搭配，意为「认知功能」。\n【竞争词】J) majestic 义为「雄伟壮丽」，与脑功能不搭\nmajestic 用来修饰山川宫殿等宏伟事物，与 function 搭配语义不通，且本段讨论的是大脑健\n康，不存在审美维度。\nK) mandatory 义为「强制的」，与「improve」宾语逻辑冲突\nmandatory function 意思变成「强制功能」，与「听莫扎特改善大脑功能」的因果链完全\n错位。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·improve + 空 + functi"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alleviate", "B) clarity", "C) cognitive", "D) composers", "E) hurt", "F) inhibiting", "G) interrogation", "H) intrinsically", "I) loose", "J) majestic", "K) mandatory", "L) recover", "M) significantly", "N) soothing", "O) wards"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) wards\n名词\n【词性槽】maternity + 空 + use…\n【依据】maternity wards 固定搭配=产科病房\nEven maternity __(31)__ use Mozart to help newborn babies 中「产科病房放莫扎特\n给新生儿听」是常识场景，wards 与 maternity 形成医院科室专名，语义、形态、搭配三\n对齐。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·maternity + 空 + use…"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alleviate", "B) clarity", "C) cognitive", "D) composers", "E) hurt", "F) inhibiting", "G) interrogation", "H) intrinsically", "I) loose", "J) majestic", "K) mandatory", "L) recover", "M) significantly", "N) soothing", "O) wards"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) significantly\n副词\n【词性槽】主谓之间修饰谓语动词的位置\n【依据】实验结论描述效果 → 显著地\nIt has been found that listening to classical music __(32)__ reduces a person's blood\npressure. 是科研结论句，常用 significantly 表达「显著降低血压」——这是研究类英语的\n高频搭配。\n【竞争词】H) intrinsically 义为「本质上」，与外在效果不搭\nintrinsically 强调「内在属性」，但 reduces a person's blood pressure 描述的是可观测的外\n部效果，逻辑指向不一致；研究语境惯用 significantly 表统计学显著。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·主谓之间修饰谓语动词的位置"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alleviate", "B) clarity", "C) cognitive", "D) composers", "E) hurt", "F) inhibiting", "G) interrogation", "H) intrinsically", "I) loose", "J) majestic", "K) mandatory", "L) recover", "M) significantly", "N) soothing", "O) wards"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) recover\n动词\n【词性槽】help your heart ___ from stress\n【依据】recover from stress=从压力中恢复\nthe calming sounds of classical music may help your heart __(33)__ from stress. 是医\n学/心理类英语固定表达，与本段「古典音乐降血压、护心」的主题完全契合。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·help your heart ___"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alleviate", "B) clarity", "C) cognitive", "D) composers", "E) hurt", "F) inhibiting", "G) interrogation", "H) intrinsically", "I) loose", "J) majestic", "K) mandatory", "L) recover", "M) significantly", "N) soothing", "O) wards"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) hurt\n动词\n【词性槽】it wouldn't ___ to do 句型，情态助动词后接动词原形\n【依据】it wouldn't hurt to do = 做……不会有坏处\nit wouldn't __(34)__ to take time out of your day to listen to music that you find\n__(35)__. 是英语固定句式「it wouldn't hurt to do sth」，意为「不妨做某事/做……没坏\n处」，与作者鼓励读者抽空听音乐的语气完全匹配。\n【竞争词】A) alleviate 及物动词，需要直接宾语，与 to do 不配\nalleviate 后必须接被缓解的对象（如 alleviate stress），而本句结构是 it wouldn't ___ to\ntake…，空后没有宾语而是不定式短语，与 alleviate 的及物用法不匹配；且 26 题已用 A。\nL) recover 义为「恢复」，与「做某事不会有坏处」语义错位\nit wouldn't recover to do 不构成英语习惯表达，recover 通常接 from / sth，且与作者「建议\n你抽空听听音乐」的语气也对不上；且 33 题已用 L。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·it wouldn't ___ to d"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alleviate", "B) clarity", "C) cognitive", "D) composers", "E) hurt", "F) inhibiting", "G) interrogation", "H) intrinsically", "I) loose", "J) majestic", "K) mandatory", "L) recover", "M) significantly", "N) soothing", "O) wards"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) soothing\n形容词\n【词性槽】find + 宾语 + 空（宾语补足语）\n【依据】music + soothing=令人舒缓的音乐\nto take time out of your day to listen to music that you find __(35)__. 与全文 The\nsounds of classical music produce a calming effect 形成首尾呼应，soothing 与\ncalming 同义，「舒缓的」音乐正是作者建议大家去听的类型。\n【竞争词】F) inhibiting 义为「抑制的」，感情色彩与劝勉语气冲突\ninhibiting 带消极色彩，意为「让人放不开/受抑制」，与作者鼓励读者享受音乐、放松身心的结\n尾语气完全相反；命题人故意把它放进来当陷阱。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 F) —— find sth + 空，形容词诱惑+词性同位。inhibiting 和\nsoothing 同为 -ing 形容词，词性都对、都能修饰 music，区别仅在感情色彩；如果不回头\n核对全文「舒缓/愉悦」的基调，很容易掉坑。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·find + 宾语 + 空（宾语补足语）"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Jackson was said to have transferred his ownership of the oak tree to itself in order to protect it from being destroyed.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】为防止被毁而转让所有权\n【定位】Towards this end, Jackson transferred by means of a deed ownership of the tree and a little\nland around it to the tree itself.\n为此，杰克逊通过一份契约的方式，把这棵树以及它周围一小片土地的所有权转让给了树\n本身。\n【改写】transferred his ownership of the oak tree to itself ↔\ntransferred by means of a deed ownership of the tree ... to the tree itself\n动作部分关键词原样复现\nin order to protect it from being destroyed ↔ his great desire to see it protected\n目的状语的同义替换 + 否定显隐\n【辨邻】 B Col. Jackson had watched the tree grow from his childhood, and grew to love it\nalmost as he would a human 解释他为何爱树，是 C 段动作的情感铺垫，但本段并\n未出现『转让所有权』这一关键动作，因而不是源头段。\nhe was pained to think that after his death it would fall into the hands of those who might destroy it", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "No proof has been found from an extensive search that Jackson had ever owned the land where the oak tree grew.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】大量检索无证据证明杰克逊拥有该地\n【定位】Further, an extensive search of land ownership records in Athens does not seem to indicate\nJackson ever owned the land the tree sits on.\n此外，对雅典市土地所有权档案的一次大规模检索似乎也没能表明杰克逊曾经拥有过那棵树\n所在的土地。\n【改写】No proof has been found from an extensive search ↔\nan extensive search of land ownership records in Athens does not seem to indicate\n『未能表明』≈『没找到证据』的隐性否定\nJackson had ever owned the land where the oak tree grew ↔\nJackson ever owned the land the tree sits on\n几乎逐词对应\n【辨邻】 L there is no hard evidence such a deed ever existed 否定的是『契约存在过』，与\n题干 Jackson had ever owned the land 否定的是『他曾拥有土地』，否定的宾语完\n全不同，因而不是源头段。\nthere is no hard evidence such a deed ever existed\nI he definitely never owned the lot the tree grew on 虽然结论同向，但该段重点在\n四英亩交易与 1200 美元，并未点出『大规模档案检索』这一题干硬锚点；因而只是局\n部相关，不是 37 题的源头段。\nhe definitely never owned the lot the tree grew on, which is the most important bit for the topic\nat hand", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "When it was raining heavily, Jackson often took shelter under a big tree that is said to own itself.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】下大雨时常在树下避雨\n【定位】Its luxuriant leaves and sturdy limbs had often protected him from the heavy rains, and out of\nits highest branches he had many a time gotten the eggs of the feathered singers.\n这棵树茂密的枝叶和粗壮的枝条常常把他从大雨中庇护下来，他还多次从最高的枝头上掏过\n鸣禽的蛋。\n【改写】When it was raining heavily ↔ from the heavy rains\n时间从句压缩成名词短语\nJackson often took shelter under a big tree ↔\nIts luxuriant leaves and sturdy limbs had often protected him\n主动『他避雨』≈ 被动『树庇护他』\n【辨邻】 C Jackson transferred by means of a deed ownership of the tree 与下雨场景毫不相\n干，C 段只共享专名 Jackson 与同一棵树，不承载题干的雨中避雨细节。\nhis great desire to see it protected has conveyed unto the said oak tree entire possession of itself", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "There is no evidence that Jackson had made arrangements to pay property taxes for the land on which the oak tree sat.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】无证据证明他为缴财产税做过安排\n【定位】Yet no account or record indicates any trust or the like was set up to facilitate this.\n然而没有任何说法或记录显示曾设立过任何信托之类的安排来落实这一点。\n【改写】There is no evidence that Jackson had made arrangements to pay property taxes ↔\nno account or record indicates any trust or the like was set up to facilitate this\n抽象的『安排』下位化为具体的『信托』\nfor the land on which the oak tree sat ↔ property taxes needed to be paid on the deeded land\n『树所在地』≈『契约所及之地』\n【辨邻】 L there is no hard evidence such a deed ever existed 否定的是契约本身的存在，与\n题干 to pay property taxes 这一税务安排无关，不是源头段。\nthere is no hard evidence such a deed ever existed", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Professor Ward paid Jackson over one thousand dollars when purchasing a piece of land from him.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Ward 教授付杰克逊一千多美元\n【定位】In the transaction, Ward was required to pay Jackson a sum of $1,200 (about $31,000 today),\neither for the property itself or simply in compensation for improvements Jackson had made\non the lot.\n在这笔交易中，Ward 被要求付给杰克逊 1200 美元（按今天的币值约 3.1 万美元），要么\n作为这处地产本身的款项，要么仅作为对杰克逊在该地块上所做改良的补偿。\n【改写】Professor Ward paid Jackson over one thousand dollars ↔\nWard was required to pay Jackson a sum of $1,200\n$1,200 落在『一千多美元』的范围里\nwhen purchasing a piece of land from him ↔\na four acre parcel ... was sold to University professor Malthus A Ward\n『从他这里买地』对应『四英亩地块卖给 Ward』\n【辨邻】 J had his little plantation seized by the Clarke County Sheriff's office and\nauctioned off to settle the mortgage 讲的是杰克逊家庄园被法警拍卖偿债，没有出\n现 Ward 也没有具体金额，与题干 Professor Ward paid Jackson 的人物方向相反，\n属于话题相邻但人物方向不符。\nhad his little plantation seized by the Clarke County Sheriff's office and auctioned off to settle\nthe mortgage", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "It is said the tree that owned itself fell in a heavy windstorm.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】该树在强风暴中倒下\n【定位】Finally, on October 9, 1942, the over 30 meter tall and 200-400 year old tree fell, rumor has\nit, as a result of a severe windstorm and/or via having previously died and its roots rotted.\n最终在 1942 年 10 月 9 日，这棵超过 30 米高、树龄 200 至 400 年的大树倒下了；传言说\n是由于一场强风暴，又或是因为它早已死去、树根烂掉所致。\n【改写】the tree that owned itself fell ↔ the over 30 meter tall and 200-400 year old tree fell\n关键动词 fell 原样复现\nin a heavy windstorm ↔ as a result of a severe windstorm\nheavy ≈ severe 的同义替换", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "The story of the oak tree is suspected to have been invented as a thought exercise.", "options": null, "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】故事被疑为思想实验\n【定位】As to why the author would do this, it's speculated perhaps it was a 19th century version of a\nclick-bait thought exercise on whether it would be legal for someone to deed such a non-\nconscious living thing to itself or not.\n至于作者为什么这么干，有人猜测，这或许是 19 世纪版的标题党式思想实验：探讨把这种\n没有意识的活物所有权授予它自己在法律上是否可行。\n【改写】The story of the oak tree is suspected to have been invented ↔\nIt is speculated to have been invented by the imagination of the said anonymous author\nsuspected ≈ speculated 的同义替换\nas a thought exercise ↔ a 19th century version of a click-bait thought exercise\nthought exercise 关键术语原样复现\n【辨邻】 M So how did all of this come about then? 只是过渡反问，没有提到 thought\nexercise，未承载题干的核心断言，因而不是源头段。\nfew give credit to this side of the story. So how did all of this come about then?", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Jackson's little plantation was auctioned off to settle his debt in the mid-19th century.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】19 世纪中叶小庄园被拍卖抵债\n【定位】Ten years later, in 1844, Jackson seemed to have come into financial difficulties and had\nhis little plantation seized by the Clarke County Sheriff's office and auctioned off to settle\nthe mortgage.\n十年后即 1844 年，杰克逊似乎陷入了财务困境，他那座小庄园被克拉克县治安官办公室没\n收并拍卖以偿还按揭。\n【改写】Jackson's little plantation was auctioned off ↔ had his little plantation ... auctioned off\n关键短语原样复现\nto settle his debt ↔ to settle the mortgage\ndebt 与 mortgage 的上下位\nin the mid-19th century ↔ Ten years later, in 1844\n1844 年落在 19 世纪中叶\n【辨邻】 I a four acre parcel ... was sold to University professor Malthus A Ward 是 1832 年\n的正常出售，既不是『auctioned off』，年份也偏早（19 世纪上半叶早期，并非中\n叶），与题干 auctioned off to settle his debt in the mid-19th century 不符。\nin 1832 a four acre parcel ... was sold to University professor Malthus A Ward", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "An official ceremony was held to celebrate the transplanting of a small tree to where its ancestor had stood.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】为小树移植到原址举行了官方仪式\n【定位】A couple of months later, an official ceremony was held featuring none other than the Mayor\nof Athens, Robert L McWhorter, to commemorate the occasion.\n几个月后还举行了一场正式仪式，由雅典市市长 Robert L McWhorter 亲自出席，以纪念\n这一时刻。\n【改写】An official ceremony was held ↔ an official ceremony was held\n关键短语原样复现\nto celebrate the transplanting of a small tree ↔\nthis little tree was transplanted to the location of its ancestor\ncelebrate 由 commemorate 同义替换；the occasion 指向移植\nwhere its ancestor had stood ↔ to the location of its ancestor\n地点状语的句式重构\n【辨邻】 F This new tree became known as The Son of the Tree That Owns Itself and it was\nassumed that, as the original tree's heir, it naturally inherited the land it stood\non 讲的是新树继承土地的故事，与题干 An official ceremony 的官方仪式不在同一事\n件，属于话题相邻而非源头段。\nThis new tree became known as The Son of the Tree That Owns Itself", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "The story of the Tree That Owns Itself appeared in the local paper several times, with slight alterations in wording.", "options": null, "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】故事在当地报纸多次出现、措辞略改\n【定位】This featured another account very clearly just copying the original article published about a\ndecade before, only slightly reworded. The next account was in 1906, again in the Athens\nWeekly Banner, again very clearly copying the original account, only slightly reworded, the\n19th century equivalent of re-posts when the audience has forgotten about the original.\n这（1901 年那一期）刊登了另一篇报道，明显只是抄录大约十年前的那篇原始报道，只是\n稍作改写。下一次出现在 1906 年，又是登在 Athens Weekly Banner 上，又是明显抄录原\n文，只是稍作改写；这相当于 19 世纪版的『读者忘了原帖后再发一遍』。\n【改写】appeared in the local paper several times ↔\nthe next known instance of the Tree That Owns Itself being mentioned wasn't until 1901 ... The\nnext account was in 1906, again in the Athens Weekly Banner\n枚举多次出现 = several times\nwith slight alterations in wording ↔ only slightly reworded\n名词化短语对应到副词 + 过去分词\n【辨邻】 N It is speculated to have been invented by the imagination of the said\nanonymous author at the Athens Weekly Banner in the aforementioned 1890\nfront page article 讨论的是文章被虚构的动机，与题干 appeared in the local paper\nseveral times 的『多次出现』不是同一事件，属于话题相邻但角度不同。\nIt is speculated to have been invented by the imagination of the said anonymous author at the\nAthens Weekly Banner\nPARAGRAPH MAP · 段落主旨图\n个段落各讲了什么\n逐段一句主旨 + 该段最具区分度的「信息指纹」，是逐句回扫定位的工作台。\nA 全文引子：介绍美国佐治亚州雅典市那棵被普遍认为拥有自己的白橡树，引出后续要\n回答的问题——它是怎么拥有自己和周围土地的。\n信息指纹 佐治亚雅典 + 拥有自己的白橡\nB 讲 19 世纪佐治亚人 William Jackson 上校如何因从小到大与这棵树的感情而想保护\n它，并直接引用 1890 年 Athens Weekly Banner 的报道原文。\n信息指纹 树为童年的杰克逊遮风挡雨、给他掏鸟蛋\nC Jackson 用一份契约把这棵树连同周围一小片土地的所有权转让给树本身，并直引契\n约内容。\n信息指纹 凭契约把所有权转给树本身\nD 这棵树成为旅游景点，20 世纪初出现衰败迹象，最终在 1942 年 10 月 9 日因疑似强\n风暴或根部腐烂而倒下。\n信息指纹 1942 年因强风暴倒下\nE 原树倒下约四年后，Junior Ladies Garden Club 找到一棵由原树果实长出的小树，\n1946 年 10 月 9 日由 Bowden 教授指导移植到原址，几个月后市长 McWhorter 出席\n了正式仪式纪念此事。\n信息指纹 1946 年移植后举行官方纪念仪式\nF 新栽下的小树被称为 The Son of the Tree That Owns Itself，作为原树的继承者继承土\n地；其他后代树尚未来争土地，本树今天仍在。\n信息指纹 新树作为原树的继承者继承土地\nG 过渡段，提出后文核心追问：Jackson 当年到底有没有真的把树的所有权合法授予树\n本身，那份契约是否具有法律效力。\n信息指纹 过渡发问：契约是否合法\nH Jackson 其实只在 1829–1832 年间在雅典住了约三年（43 岁起），这削弱了他从小爱\n树的说法；土地档案大量检索也没显示他曾拥有那棵树所在的土地。\n信息指纹 档案大量检索找不到杰克逊拥有树所在土地的证据\nI Jackson 确实曾住在树旁一块地上三年，但是否拥有不清楚；1832 年含树所在地与他\n所住邻地的四英亩地块卖给了 Ward 教授，交易里 Ward 须付 Jackson 1200 美元，无\n论如何 Jackson 都没拥有树所在的那块地。\n信息指纹 Ward 在交易中付给杰克逊 1200 美元\nJ\n购地之后 Jackson 全家搬到几英里外一块 655 英亩的庄园；十年后即 1844 年陷入财\n务困境，小庄园被 Clarke County 治安官没收并拍卖以偿还按揭。\n信息指纹 1844 年小庄园被治安官拍卖偿债\nK 即便契约真的存在，Jackson 也应当知道要替这块树所在的地缴纳财产税才能让树真\n正得到保障；然而没有任何记录显示他为此设立过信托之类的安排。\n信息指纹 没有为缴纳财产税设立过信托等安排\nL 更要命的是，雅典市的契约档案虽追溯到 Jackson 1876 年去世前数十年，却找不到这\n样一份契约存在过的硬证据，尽管 1890 年那位匿名记者声称在档案里见过它。\n信息指纹 档案中找不到该契约存在的硬证据\nM 过渡段：说基于上述质疑，很少有人相信那一面的故事，再次反问这一切是怎么传出\n来的。\n信息指纹 过渡反问：这故事是如何起源的\nN 推测整个故事是 1890 年那篇匿名头版文章 Deeded to Itself 的作者凭想象虚构的，\n可能是 19 世纪版的标题党式思想实验：探讨能否把没有意识的活物所有权授予它\n自己。\n信息指纹 被猜测为标题党式思想实验\nO 讲这故事接下来在同一份报纸上的再现：1901 年百年特刊里复述一遍（只是稍作改\n写），1906 年又登一次，同样只是稍作改写，相当于 19 世纪版的重发帖。\n信息指纹 故事在同一报纸多次以微改措辞重发", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What is one of the factors contributing to failure of intervention in face of risky behavior in the workplace?", "options": ["A) Slack supervision style.", "B) Unfavorable workplace culture.", "C) Unforeseeable risk.", "D) Blocked communication."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What is one of the factors contributing to failure of intervention in face of risky\nbehavior in the workplace? 用 What 直接问一个具体因素，答案落点为原文明确给出的某\n项 factor，是典型客观细节题。\n【拆句】主干为 What is one of the factors，后接现在分词短语 contributing to failure of\nintervention 作定语修饰 factors，再用介词短语 in face of risky behavior in the\nworkplace 限定场景。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Second, the company's culture needs to make them feel safe to speak up.\n第二，公司的文化需要让他们觉得发声是安全的。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nP2 列举的三个因素中并无 Slack supervision style（监管松懈）。supervisors 在 P4\nP8 才出现，且讨论的是是否亲自干预，与「监管风格松懈」无关。\nB) ✓ 正确\nthe company's culture needs to make them feel safe to speak up ↔\nUnfavorable workplace culture\n原文说「需要文化让人敢说」，预判答案是「文化不让人敢说」，B 项 Unfavorable\nworkplace culture 正是这一缺失态的同义改写，因此锁定 B。\nC) 与原文相反\nthey need to be able to see a risky situation beginning to unfold 说的是「员工要\n能预见风险」，而 Unforeseeable risk 反过来说「风险无法预见」，与原文方向相反；\n且不在 P2 的三因素列表中。\nD) 偷换概念\nthey need to have the communication skills to say something effectively 说的是\n「需要具备沟通技能」，强调的是个人技能层面；而 Blocked communication 说「沟\n通被阻塞」，把「技能不足」偷换为「通道被堵」，对象完全不同。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What does the author mean by \"diffusion of responsibility\" (Para. 3)?", "options": ["A) The more people are around, the more they need to worry about their personal safety.", "B) The more people who witness an event, the less likely anyone will venture to", "C) The more people idling around on the street, the more likely they need taking care of.", "D) The more people are around, the less chance someone will step forward to intervene."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （词义句意题）\n【判型】What does the author mean by 是经典的释义提问框架，答案为原文对该术语的同义\n解释。\n【拆句】主干 What does the author mean by，by 后跟引号词作宾语，括号内 (Para. 3) 为定位提\n示。结构清晰，无陷阱。\n【定位】Basically, the larger the crowd, the more people assume that someone else will take care of it\n—meaning no one effectively intervenes or acts in a moment of need.\n基本上，人群越大，越多人就假定别人会处理这事——也就是说，在需要出手时反而无人有\n效干预或行动。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nthey need to worry about their personal safety 把原文「无人干预他人」的话题偷换\n成「担心自身安全」，自身安全在 P3 里根本未提及。\nB) 答非所问\nThe more people who witness an event, the less likely anyone will venture to 后\n半句 venture to 后无宾语，句子残缺；即便补全也只是 the more people who\nwitness an event, the less likely anyone in that group is to help the victim 的\nbystander effect 释义，而题干问的是 diffusion of responsibility 这一更深层心理机\n制，对应的是 P3 后半句而非这句。\nC) 偷换概念\npeople idling around on the street 把原文 stand idly by 的「袖手旁观者」读成「街\n上闲逛的人」，并称他们「需要被照顾」，与 diffusion of responsibility 的心理机制\n无关。\nD) ✓ 正确\nthe larger the crowd, the more people assume that someone else will take care of it ↔\nThe more people are around, the less chance someone will step forward to intervene\n选项 D 的两段比较级与原文的两段比较级一一对应：人多→人多假定→更少人出手干\n预，与预判「越多越没人挺身而出」严丝合缝，故锁定 D。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "词义句意题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What happens when unsafe behavior at the workplace is not addressed by the leaders?", "options": ["A) No one will intervene when they see similar behaviors.", "B) Everyone will see it as the easiest way to deal with crisis.", "C) Workers have to take extra caution executing their duties.", "D) Workers are left to take care of the emergency themselves."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What happens when unsafe behavior at the workplace is not addressed by the\nleaders? 问「当条件 X 成立时结果是什么」，答案为原文明文给出的客观后果。\n【拆句】主干 What happens when，when 引导时间/条件状语从句，从句主语 unsafe behavior\nat the workplace，谓语为被动语态 is not addressed by the leaders。注意被动否定结\n构，主体是「领导层不去处理」。\n【定位】When a group of employees sees unsafe behavior not being addressed at a leadership level it\ncreates the precedent that this is how these situations should be addressed, thus defining the\nsafety culture for everyone.\n当一群员工看到领导层不处理不安全行为时，就会形成「这类情况就该这样处理」的先例，\n从而为所有人定义了安全文化。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nit creates the precedent that this is how these situations should be addressed ↔\nNo one will intervene when they see similar behaviors\n结合上下文（领导没干预+形成先例），先例的具体内容就是「对类似行为不干预」，选\n项 A 正是该先例的客观转述，故锁定 A。\nB) 过度推断\n原文只说形成「该这样处理」的先例，并未评价它是 the easiest way；且「危机处理」\n角度也是 crisis 强加的描述。\nC) 无中生有\nWorkers have to take extra caution 在原文中找不到根据，原文是说大家集体不干\n预，并非「工人变得更小心」。\nD) 与原文相反\nWorkers are left to take care of the emergency themselves 说工人会自己去处理紧\n急情况，而 P5 的逻辑是「没人去处理」，方向完全相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What is the ultimate reason workers won't act when they see unsafe operations?", "options": ["A) Preference of deferring the action to others.", "B) Anticipation of leadership intervention.", "C) Fear of being isolated by coworkers.", "D) Fear of having to do more work."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What is the ultimate reason workers won't act when they see unsafe operations? 直\n问最终原因，答案为原文一句明文判断，非推断。\n【拆句】主干 What is the ultimate reason，后接省略了 why/that 的定语从句 workers won't\nact，再嵌套时间状语从句 when they see unsafe operations。注意「won't act」等价于\n「don't intervene」。\n【定位】Fear is the ultimate factor in not intervening. There is a fear of penalty, a fear that they'll have\nto do more work if they intervene.\n恐惧是不干预的最终因素。存在对惩罚的恐惧，以及对「一旦干预就得做更多工作」的\n恐惧。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\nPreference of deferring the action to others 对应 they tend to prefer to defer that\naction to someone else for all future situations，但这是 Unsuccessful attempts in\nthe past（过去失败经验）的副产品，是 another strong contributing factor，不是\nultimate factor。\nB) 无中生有\nAnticipation of leadership intervention 在 P6 没有依据。文中 P4 P5 提到\nleadership 不干预，但谈的是「领导自己不出手」形成先例，而非「员工等领导\n干预」。\nC) 无中生有\nFear of being isolated by coworkers 中「被同事孤立」在 P6 罗列的几种 fear\n（penalty / do more work / unsuccessful attempts）中并无对应。\nD) ✓ 正确\na fear that they'll have to do more work if they intervene ↔ Fear of having to do more work\n原文罗列了两类 fear，选项 D 把第二类「怕多干活」原样收纳，与预判完全一致，故锁\n定 D。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What is critical to ensuring workplace safety?", "options": ["A) Workers be trained to operate their equipment properly.", "B) Workers exhibiting at-risk behavior be strictly disciplined.", "C) Supervisors create a safety environment for timely intervention.", "D) Supervisors conduct effective communication with frontline workers."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What is critical to ensuring workplace safety? 问「对确保职场安全至关重要的是什\n么」，答案为原文显式给出的某项做法，非主旨概括。\n【拆句】主干 What is critical to ensuring workplace safety?，critical to 后接动名词短语\nensuring workplace safety 作介词宾语。注意 to 是介词不是不定式，故跟动名词。结构无\n陷阱。\n【定位】Supervisors also play a critical role. Even if a competent person isn't required, supervisors\nneed a broad set of skills to not only identify and alleviate workplace hazards but also\nbuild a safety climate within their team that supports intervening and open communication\namong them.\n主管也起着至关重要的作用。即便不需要专门的胜任人员，主管也需要一整套技能，既要能\n识别和消除职场隐患，也要在团队中营造支持干预和坦诚沟通的安全氛围。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nWorkers be trained to operate their equipment properly 中「培训工人正确操作设\n备」在原文中无对应；without the proper equipment 谈的是「没有合适设备」，与\n「培训操作设备」不是一回事。\nB) 过度推断\nWorkers exhibiting at-risk behavior be strictly disciplined 中「对有风险行为的工人\n进行严厉惩戒」，P6 只是说工人怕 penalty，不代表全文主张要 strictly discipline，原\n文并未把「严厉惩戒」作为确保安全的做法。\nC) ✓ 正确\nbuild a safety climate within their team that supports intervening ↔\nSupervisors create a safety environment for timely intervention\n主语对位（Supervisors↔Supervisors）、动作对位（build↔create）、对象对位\n（safety climate↔safety environment）、目的对位（supports intervening↔for\ntimely intervention），与预判一致，故锁定 C。\nD) 以偏概全\nSupervisors conduct effective communication with frontline workers 抓住了 open\ncommunication，但漏掉原文核心 build a safety climate within their team that\nsupports intervening——主管的关键不仅是沟通，而是建立支持干预的安全氛围；且\ncommunication with frontline workers 把原文 open communication among them\n（团队内部沟通）偷换为「主管与一线工人之间的沟通」，对象错位。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What accounts for the divide within the scientific community about nuclear energy?", "options": ["A) Attention to combating human-made climate change.", "B) Emphasis on protecting wildlife and natural ecosystems.", "C) Evolution of the term \"green energy\" over the last century.", "D) Adherence to different interpretations of environmentalism."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What accounts for the divide within the scientific community about nuclear energy?\n问的是\"分裂的原因是什么\"，是对原文具体因果关系的客观追问。\n【拆句】Q51 主语是疑问词 What，谓语 accounts for（=解释/导致），宾语是 the divide within\nthe scientific community about nuclear energy，about 短语限定 divide 的具体领域。\n结构清晰、无陷阱。\n【定位】The distinction between these two strands of environmentalism is the cause of a split within\nthe scientific community about nuclear energy.\n这两种环保主义之间的区别，正是科学界在核能问题上发生分裂的原因。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nAttention to combating human-made climate change 只对应 P1 里\nenvironmentalist 的\"第二种含义\"，不是\"两种含义的差异\"。\nB) 以偏概全\nEmphasis on protecting wildlife and natural ecosystems 只对应 P1 里\nenvironmentalist 的\"第一种含义\"，同样只覆盖单侧。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文谈的是 The term \"environmentalist\" 在 21 世纪的演变，Evolution of the term\n\"green energy\" over the last century 把术语换成了 green energy、时间换成了 the\nlast century。\nD) ✓ 正确\nThe distinction between these two strands of environmentalism ↔\nAdherence to different interpretations of environmentalism\n预判要求答案体现\"对环保主义的不同理解→科学界分裂\"这一因果链，Adherence\nto different interpretations of environmentalism 是四项中唯一精准覆盖该因果的\n表述。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What is the solution to energy shortage proposed by purists' opponents?", "options": ["A) Relying on renewables firmly and exclusively.", "B) Using fossil fuel and green energy alternately.", "C) Opting for nuclear energy when necessary.", "D) Limiting people's non-basic consumption."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What is the solution to energy shortage proposed by purists\\' opponents? 问对立方\n提出的具体方案，是原文已有的客观表述。\n【拆句】Q52 主干 What is the solution，to energy shortage 修饰 solution，过去分词短语\nproposed by purists\\' opponents 后置修饰 solution。注意 purists\\' 是所有格，\nopponents 才是真正的主语方。\n【定位】The opposing side agrees that renewables are crucial, but says society needs an amount of\npower available to meet consumers' basic demands when the sun isn't shining and the wind\nisn't blowing. Nuclear energy, being far cleaner than oil, gas and coal, is a natural option,\nespecially where hydroelectric capacity is limited.\n对立方认同可再生能源关键，但表示在阳光不足、无风时社会仍需一定量电力以满足消费者\n的基本需求。核能比油、气、煤都清洁得多，是自然之选，尤其在水电能力有限的地方。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nRelying on renewables firmly and exclusively 描述的恰恰是 purists 的立场，而非对\n立方——对立方主张的是核能补缺，绝不\"独占\"可再生能源。\nB) 无中生有\nUsing fossil fuel and green energy alternately 谈\"化石燃料和绿色能源交替使用\"，\n原文从未提出过\"交替使用\"这一方案，且对立方明确说核能 far cleaner than oil, gas\nand coal，即反对烧煤气油。\nC) ✓ 正确\nNuclear energy, being far cleaner than oil, gas and coal, is a natural option, especially where\nhydroelectric capacity is limited\n↔ Opting for nuclear energy when necessary\n预判结论\"必要时启用核能补缺\"在四项中只有 Opting for nuclear energy when\nnecessary 精准匹配。\nD) 无中生有\nLimiting people\\'s non-basic consumption 谈\"限制非基本消费\"，原文只提\nconsumers\\' basic demands——\"满足基本需求\"和\"限制非基本消费\"是两回事，后者\n属凭空引申。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What point does the author want to make with cities like Burlington as an example?", "options": ["A) It is controversial whether the goal of the whole world's exclusive dependence on renewables is attainable.", "B) It is contentious whether cities with large populations have renewable resources at their immediate disposal.", "C) It is arguable whether cities that manage to run off renewables have sustainable hydroelectric capabilities.", "D) It is debatable whether traditional fossil fuel energy can be done away with entirely throughout the world."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （例证目的题）\n【判型】What point does the author want to make with cities like Burlington as an example?\n直接问\"举这个例子是为了说明什么\"，是典型例证目的题。\n【拆句】Q53 主干 What point does the author want to make，介词短语 with cities like\nBurlington as an example 作伴随状语，说明作者作出该 point 时所凭借的例子。with…\nas… 是\"以……作为……\"的固定结构。\n【定位】The achievability of universal 100% renewability is similarly contentious. Cities such as\nBurlington, Vermont, have been \"100% renewable\" for years.\n全球 100% 可再生能源是否能实现同样有争议。像佛蒙特州 Burlington 这样的城市多年来\n一直号称\"100% 可再生\"。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nThe achievability of universal 100% renewability is similarly contentious ↔\nIt is controversial whether the goal of the whole world\\'s exclusive dependence on\nrenewables is attainable\n预判结论包含三要素\"全球范围、独占可再生、可达成性存疑\"，A 是四项中唯一同时覆\n盖三要素的表述。\nB) 偷换概念\ncities with large populations have renewable resources at their immediate\ndisposal 谈\"大人口城市是否有可再生资源\"，原文 P6 只说 Burlington 这类\"小人口\"城\n市，并未把议题转向\"大人口城市能否做到\"。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文 countries that manage to run off renewables typically do so thanks to\nextraordinary hydroelectric capabilities 只说\"靠可再生运转的国家\"通常水电禀赋强，\n并未质疑其\"可持续性\"；选项 sustainable hydroelectric capabilities 把客观描述偷换\n为\"是否可持续\"的争议。\nD) 过度推断\ntraditional fossil fuel energy can be done away with entirely throughout the\nworld 谈\"是否能在全球范围彻底废除化石燃料\"，原文只论\"是否能 100% 可再生\"，并\n未直接讨论\"彻底废除化石燃料\"，两者虽相关但不等价。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "例证目的题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What do we learn about Germany regarding renewable energy?", "options": ["A) It has increased its wind and solar power generation four times over the last two decades.", "B) It represents a good example of a major industrialized country promoting green energy.", "C) It relies on renewable energy to generate more than half of its electricity.", "D) It has succeeded in reaching the goal of energy transition set by Merkel."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn about Germany regarding renewable energy? 是对原文中关于德国\n可再生能源相关事实的复核问题。\n【拆句】Q54 主干 What do we learn about Germany，regarding renewable energy 介词短语\n限定话题范围（regarding=关于）。句式平铺，无陷阱。\n【定位】Germany stands as the best case study for a large, industrialized country pushing into\ngreen energy.\n德国是大型工业化国家推进绿色能源的最佳案例。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文 Wind and solar power generation has increased over 400% since 2010 是\"自\n2010 年以来\"约 10 年，而 over the last two decades 是\"过去二十年\"——时间维度被\n夸大一倍。\nB) ✓ 正确\nGermany stands as the best case study for a large, industrialized country pushing into green\nenergy\n↔ It represents a good example of a major industrialized country promoting green energy\n预判结论\"大型工业国推进绿能的范本\"与 B 在主谓宾每个成分上都做到同义替换，是最\n高质量的同义匹配。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文 renewables provided 46% of the country\\'s electricity in 2019 是 46%，more\nthan half 是\">50%\"——46% 显然不到一半，是数字偷换。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文只说默克尔 2011 年宣布 Energiewende，未说德国\"已成功达成\"该目标；事实上\nBut progress has halted in recent years 直接表明进展停滞，说明远未达成。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What may be one of the reasons for Germany's progress having halted in recent years?", "options": ["A) Its grid infrastructure's capacity has fallen behind its development of green energy.", "B) Its overabundance of green energy has forced power plants to suspend operation during daytime.", "C) Its industrial south is used to running factories on conventional energy supplies.", "D) Its renewable energy supplies are unstable both at night and during the day."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What may be one of the reasons for Germany\\'s progress having halted in recent\nyears? 问\"原因之一\"，对应原文列出多条原因中的一条，本质是细节复述。\n【拆句】Q55 主干 What may be one of the reasons，介词短语 for Germany\\'s progress\nhaving halted 后置修饰 reasons，其中 having halted 是动名词完成式作 progress 的语\n义谓语，表明\"进展已经停滞\"这件事。in recent years 是时间状语。\n【定位】Lagging grid infrastructure struggles to transport this overabundance of green energy from\nGermany's north to its industrial south, meaning many factories still run on coal and gas.\n滞后的电网基础设施难以把过剩的绿电从德国北部输送到工业化南部，这意味着许多工厂仍\n靠煤气运转。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nLagging grid infrastructure struggles to transport this overabundance of green energy ↔\nIts grid infrastructure\\'s capacity has fallen behind its development of green energy\n预判结论\"电网跟不上绿电规模\"在四项中只有 A 精准命中，主语\"grid infrastructure\"与\n原文同名重合，谓语\"has fallen behind\"与 Lagging 同义。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文 solar and wind can overwhelm the grid during the day, forcing utilities to\npay customers to use their electricity 是\"电网超负荷迫使电力公司付钱让顾客用电\"，\nforced power plants to suspend operation during daytime 把\"付钱让顾客用电\"偷\n换为\"电厂停运\"，主语和动作均失实。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文 many factories still run on coal and gas 是\"由于电网无法把北部绿电输到南\n部\"这一被动结果；Its industrial south is used to running factories on conventional\nenergy supplies 把它说成\"南部工业区习惯于用传统能源\"——把客观限制偷换为主观\n习惯。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文 The instability of renewables doesn\\'t just mean energy is often not\nproduced at night, but also that solar and wind can overwhelm the grid during\nthe day 是\"不仅夜里没电、白天还过剩\"——表面看选项 D 似乎和原文一致，但\nunstable both at night and during the day 把\"夜间缺供+白天过剩\"这一具体二元情\n况偷换为笼统的\"昼夜都不稳定\"，丢失了关键差异。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
